     (restore-fn base entry))))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:428:(defn park-continuation\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:442:(defn resume-continuation\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:444:  [state parked-id resume-val restore-fn]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:447:      (-> (restore-fn new-state parked resume-val)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:477:(defn handle-effect\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:479:   park-entry-fns maps :stream/put and :stream/next to functions that build\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:488:  [state effect {:keys [park-entry-fns], :as opts}]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:489:  (let [park-entry (get park-entry-fns (:effect effect))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:78:(declare semantic-restore scheduler-round)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:105:(defn- response-wait-entry\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:108:   fields, in the shape `ffi/call-response-wait-entry` gives the walker.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:134:                            (response-wait-entry (:segment entry)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:181:   No `:restore-fn` crosses this boundary. A parked entry is pure data and\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:184:  [vm seg pc val St E K effect park-entry-fns]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:188:                              (when park-entry-fns\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:189:                                {:park-entry-fns park-entry-fns}))]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:219:                {:park-entry-fns (call-park-entries seg pc E St K)})]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:242:    (scheduler-round vm)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:434:                                 (response-wait-entry seg (inc pc) E St' K\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:471:(defn- scheduler-round\n     1\t# yin.vm engine: the VM seam\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: contract of the existing `yin.vm.engine` namespace as of 2026-09-22,\n     4\tplus three additive engine edits proposed for B4 of\n     5\t`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`. No protocol is introduced; see section 6.\n     6\t\n     7\t`yin.vm.engine` is the shared scheduler for every Yin VM: the dispatch loop,\n     8\twait-set polling, ready-queue resumption, park and resume records, and the\n     9\tstream-effect handlers. It is VM-agnostic because every place it needs to\n    10\tknow a VM's register layout it takes a function argument. This document\n    11\tnames that seam precisely so the third VM (B4) and any later one (R4) are\n    12\twritten against a stated contract rather than by copying the last VM.\n    13\t\n    14\t## 1. What the engine owns\n    15\t\n    16\tFunctions, all already VM-shape-agnostic:\n    17\t\n    18\t    run-loop                state active? step-fn resume-fn -> state\n    19\t    check-wait-set          state -> state          (poll, wake onto ready-queue)\n    20\t    resume-from-run-queue   state restore-fn -> state | nil\n    21\t    park-continuation       state cont-fields -> state\n    22\t    resume-continuation     state parked-id val restore-fn -> state\n    23\t    handle-effect           state effect {:park-entry-fns ...} -> outcome map\n    24\t    gensym, handle-make/put/cursor/next/close, handle-stream-block\n    25\t\n    26\tBookkeeping keys the engine reads or writes on the VM state map. A VM that\n    27\tmaintains these gets `run-loop`, `active-continuation?`,\n    28\t`halted-with-empty-queue?`, `vm-blocked?`, and `vm-value` for free:\n    29\t\n    30\t    :blocked?  :halted?  :wait-set  :ready-queue  :parked\n    31\t    :id-counter  :store  :value  :make-stream  :modules\n    32\t\n    33\tKnown vocabulary leak: `ready-for-ingress?` also reads `:k`, `:control`, and\n    34\t`:bytecode`, the named VMs' register names. It is the observer's idle gate,\n    35\tnot scheduler machinery, and a VM without those keys that is ever placed\n    36\tunder `dao.stream.observer` must supply its own idle predicate. B4 does not\n    37\tconsume it.\n    38\t\n    39\tThe program-cache helpers (`pinned-compiled-versions` and friends) walk\n    40\t`:parked`, `:ready-queue`, and `:wait-set` looking for `:compiled-version`\n    41\tinside `:next`-chained frames. On a payload without those keys they find\n    42\tnothing, which is correct; no VM in this repository uses the compiled cache.\n    43\t\n    44\t## 2. What a VM supplies\n    45\t\n    46\tExactly one VM-level function and one instruction-site builder shape.\n    47\t\n    48\t### 2.1 The restore function\n    49\t\n    50\t    restore : base entry val -> state\n    51\t\n    52\t`base` is the VM state after the engine has done its part: the ready entry\n    53\tpopped, `:store-updates` merged, `:blocked?` and `:halted?` cleared (from\n    54\t`resume-from-run-queue`), or the parked record removed (from\n    55\t`resume-continuation`). `entry` is the wait, ready, or parked record. `val`\n    56\tis the value the parked instruction receives: the woken stream value, or the\n    57\tresume operand.\n    58\t\n    59\tThe restore writes the VM's registers from the entry's register keys and\n    60\tplaces `val` where the instruction after the park point expects its result.\n    61\tIt leaves the bookkeeping keys as `base` has them. The one exception is the\n    62\tFFI two-step in section 4, which may re-park.\n    63\t\n    64\tEvery restore ever written in this repository has this signature: the\n    65\twalker's `ast-walker-restore`, the semantic VM's `semantic-restore`, and\n    66\tv1's `semantic-vm-restore`, `stack-vm-restore`, and `register-vm-restore`\n    67\t(deleted in `d8b27a50`). The only variation is a two-arity shim\n    68\t`([base entry] (restore base entry (:value entry)))` that exists because\n    69\t`resume-from-run-queue` calls the function with two arguments and\n    70\t`resume-continuation` with three. That is a defect of the engine's call\n    71\tconvention, not a VM choice, and section 7 removes it.\n    72\t\n    73\t### 2.2 The park-entry builder\n    74\t\n    75\t    park-entry : state effect result -> entry\n    76\t\n    77\tHanded to `handle-effect` under `:park-entry-fns` keyed by `:stream/put` and\n    78\t`:stream/next`. It returns the VM's register payload for the continuation\n    79\tafter the blocking instruction, merged with the transport keys the engine\n    80\treads:\n    81\t\n    82\t    (merge registers {:reason :put   :stream-id id})\n    83\t    (merge registers {:reason :next  :stream-id id :cursor-ref ref})\n    84\t\n    85\t`handle-effect` stamps `:datom` onto a `:put` entry if the builder did not.\n    86\t\n    87\tBuilders close over instruction-site locals: the semantic VM's over\n    88\t`seg pc E St K` in its hot loop, the walker's over `k env`. They are\n    89\tinstruction-level values, not VM-level ones, which is why no protocol on the\n    90\tVM type can express them and why they stay as closures at the call site.\n    91\t\n    92\t## 3. The entry key contract\n    93\t\n    94\tThis is the actual interface between the engine and a VM. A wait, ready, or\n    95\tparked entry is the union of two disjoint key sets.\n    96\t\n    97\tEngine-owned keys, read or written by the engine and `dao.stream.waitset`:\n    98\t\n    99\t    :reason  :stream-id  :cursor-ref  :datom\n   100\t    :value  :status  :store-updates  :cursor\n   101\t    :type  :id                           (parked records only)\n   102\t\n   103\t`:stream` may appear on a woken entry transiently and is dropped before the\n   104\tentry reaches the ready queue.\n   105\t\n   106\tFFI keys, owned by the `yin.vm.ffi` convention: `:call-id`, `:request-sent`,\n   107\t`:op`.\n   108\t\n   109\tEvery other key is the VM's register payload. The engine never reads,\n   110\twrites, or removes it, and it survives park, poll, wake, and resume\n   111\tverbatim. A VM's register keys must not collide with the sets above.\n   112\t\n   113\tPayloads in use or planned:\n   114\t\n   115\t    +-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   116\t    | VM                    | Register payload                              |\n   117\t    +-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   118\t    | yin.vm.ast_walker     | {:k :env}                                     |\n   119\t    | yin.vm.semantic       | {:segment :pc :env :stack :k}                 |\n   120\t    | yin.vm.debruijn.stack | {:segment :pc :frames :stack :continuation}   |\n   121\t    |   (B4, planned)       |                                               |\n   122\t    | register kernel (R4,  | its own; same rules                           |\n   123\t    |   gated, hypothetical)|                                               |\n   124\t    +-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   125\t\n   126\tTwo value tags are also shared, because the B0 normalizer compares\n   127\tcontinuations and parked records by type only: a reified continuation is\n   128\t`{:type :reified-continuation ...registers}` and a parked record is\n   129\t`{:type :parked-continuation :id ...registers}` (the engine adds the tag and\n   130\tid in `park-continuation`).\n   131\t\n   132\t## 4. The FFI two-step\n   133\t\n   134\tAn `:ffi-call` parks, appends a request, and then waits twice: first, if the\n   135\tcall-in stream was `full`, as a writer retrying the request; then as a reader\n   136\ton the call-out cursor for the correlated response. Two entry shapes result:\n   137\t\n   138\t- A woken `:request-sent` writer must not resume. Its registers are re-parked\n   139\t  as a call-out reader with `:call-id`, and the machine stays blocked.\n   140\t- A woken `:call-id` reader unwraps its value with `ffi/call-result`, drops\n   141\t  its record from `:parked`, and resumes with the unwrapped value.\n   142\t\n   143\tThe two live VMs place this logic differently. The semantic VM handles both\n   144\tcases inside `semantic-restore`, keyed on top-level entry keys. The walker\n   145\thandles them in its continuation dispatch, keyed on frame types\n   146\t`:dao.stream.apply/request-sent` and `:dao.stream.apply/eval-call` that\n   147\t`ffi/call-response-wait-entry` builds into `:k`. Both are correct; they are\n   148\ttwo placements of the same fifteen lines.\n   149\t\n   150\tB4 uses the semantic VM's placement. The stack VM's continuation is a vector\n   151\tof return frames, not typed CESK frames, so the walker's placement does not\n   152\tfit it. Under the section 3 rule the re-park is register-agnostic: the\n   153\tresponse reader is the writer entry with `:request-sent`, `:op`, and `:datom`\n   154\tremoved and the call-out reader keys added. Section 7 adds that helper.\n   155\t\n   156\tThe two-step is not lifted into the engine now. Doing so would require the\n   157\twalker to abandon its frame-typed placement, which is a refactor of a working\n   158\tVM that B4 does not need. It is the one piece of genuinely duplicated logic\n   159\tat this seam, and it becomes a three-instance extraction candidate once B4\n   160\texists. The section 7 helper makes that extraction mechanical.\n   161\t\n   162\t## 5. Glue that is pure duplication\n   163\t\n   164\tEach VM today writes, in addition to its restore and its builders:\n   165\t\n   166\t    +----------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n   167\t    | Per-VM glue                | Why it exists                             |\n   168\t    +----------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n   169\t    | private resume-from-run-   | engine has no form with restore-fn bound  |\n   170\t    |   queue wrapper (1 line)   |                                           |\n   171\t    | scheduler-round: check-    | engine has no composed form; the walker   |\n   172\t    |   wait-set then resume     | inlines it, the semantic VM names it      |\n   173\t    | two-arity restore shim     | engine calls restore with 2 and 3 args    |\n   174\t    | X-run-scheduler: run-loop  | fine; stays (it binds step-fn, which is   |\n   175\t    |   with step-fn bound       | genuinely per-VM)                         |\n   176\t    +----------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n   177\t\n   178\tThe first three are eliminated by the section 7 edits. What remains per VM\n   179\tis exactly what must be per VM: the restore, the builders, and a one-line\n   180\trunner binding the VM's step function.\n   181\t\n   182\t## 6. Why not a protocol\n   183\t\n   184\tThe question was whether to formalize the restore-fn and park-entry contract\n   185\tas a protocol, a multimethod, or a required key on the VM state map.\n   186\t\n   187\tThe answer is no, and not because there is only one instance to generalize\n   188\tfrom. That premise is false: two VMs use this seam in production today\n   189\t(`yin.vm.ast_walker` and `yin.vm.semantic`), three did in v1 (semantic,\n   190\tstack, register, all against a v1 engine of the same shape), and in none of\n   191\tthe five did the restore signature vary. The 3-VM case this proposal worries\n   192\tabout was already run, in this codebase, on the bare-function convention,\n   193\twithout a protocol, and the convention held. That is a demonstrated outcome,\n   194\tnot a prediction.\n   195\t\n   196\tWhat the evidence shows is that the VM-specific surface is one function of\n   197\tfixed signature plus builder closures that no VM-level dispatch can reach. A\n   198\tprotocol would replace one positional argument with type dispatch, add an\n   199\t`extend-type` obligation to every VM, and break the engine tests' plain-map\n   200\tstates and fake restore functions, in exchange for nothing the argument does\n   201\tnot already do. A `:yin.vm.engine/restore` key holding the function on the\n   202\tstate map is worse: it puts a closure in VM state, which the v2 port\n   203\tspecifically removed (\"a ready entry holds registers, ids, and values, never\n   204\ta closure\") so that a blocked machine survives an EDN round-trip.\n   205\t\n   206\tThe v1 to v2 history points the same way. v1 threaded `restore-fn` into\n   207\t`handle-effect`, `check-wait-set`, and `run-loop`, and wrapped entries as\n   208\truntime tasks. v2 deleted all of that and left restore dispatch in exactly\n   209\ttwo engine functions. The engine has been getting less abstract at this\n   210\tseam, on purpose. `datom.world.md`: interpretation over abstraction, simple\n   211\tdata over rich types.\n   212\t\n   213\tWhat was missing is not a mechanism but a statement: which keys the engine\n   214\towns, what the restore signature is, and the two composed forms every VM\n   215\twas writing by hand. This document is the statement; section 7 is the two\n   216\tforms.\n   217\t\n   218\t## 7. Proposed engine edits (B4's file box)\n   219\t\n   220\tAll additive, all inside `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc` and\n   221\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc`, none changing an effect rule or an outcome:\n   222\t\n   223\t1. `engine/scheduler-round [state restore-fn]`: `check-wait-set`, then\n   224\t   `resume-from-run-queue` with `restore-fn`, returning the polled state when\n   225\t   nothing woke. This is `yin.vm.semantic/scheduler-round` moved verbatim\n   226\t   with its restore made a parameter.\n   227\t\n   228\t2. One restore arity. `resume-from-run-queue` calls\n   229\t   `(restore-fn base entry (:value entry))` so that both engine call sites\n   230\t   pass three arguments. The existing two-arity shims in the semantic VM and\n   231\t   the walker keep working and may be deleted later; the fake restore in\n   232\t   `test/yin/vm/engine_test.cljc` gains a third parameter.\n   233\t\n   234\t3. `ffi/response-wait-entry [entry call-id]`: the call-out reader entry for\n   235\t   an arbitrary register payload, built by removing `:request-sent`, `:op`,\n   236\t   and `:datom` and adding `:call-id`, `:reason :next`, the call-out\n   237\t   `:cursor-ref`, and `:stream-id`. `ffi/call-response-wait-entry` (the\n   238\t   walker's frame-typed shape) is unchanged.\n   239\t\n   240\tTwo tests: `scheduler-round` returns the polled state when nothing wakes and\n   241\tthe restored state when something does; `response-wait-entry` preserves an\n   242\tarbitrary register payload verbatim.\n   243\t\n   244\tB4 consumes all three. The semantic VM and the walker are not migrated in\n   245\tB4: they are B4's parity oracles and must not move while B4 is measured\n   246\tagainst them. Migrating them afterwards is a separate, behavior-preserving\n   247\tcommit (delete `scheduler-round`, `resume-from-run-queue`, and\n   248\t`response-wait-entry` from `semantic.cljc`; delete `resume-from-run-queue`\n   249\tand the inline round from `ast_walker.cljc`) and is the owner's call.\n   250\t\n   251\t## 8. Findings outside B4's scope\n   252\t\n   253\t- `yin.vm.ast_walker` passes `{:restore-fn ast-walker-restore}` into\n   254\t  `engine/handle-effect` at six sites. The v2 engine destructures only\n   255\t  `:park-entry-fns` and ignores it. This is v1 plumbing that survived the\n   256\t  port; `yin.vm.semantic.md` section 3.5 states the rule the walker does not\n   257\t  yet follow. Harmless, and B4 must not copy it.\n   258\t- `ready-for-ingress?` speaks the named register vocabulary (section 1).\n   259\t- The semantic VM's private `response-wait-entry` becomes redundant with\n   260\t  edit 3 above.\n   210\tis attached to the image, request, response, verification, or cache. Any later\n   211\tcache is keyed by H.\n   212\tNo cache is specified by B0 through B5; any later cache is an explicit value\n   213\tkeyed by H. A receiver hashes the received canonical wire bytes before\n   214\tdecoding; those bytes are H's preimage. `jing/segment-key` is only the storage\n   215\taddress for the same bytes and is not H; `yin.vm.content` stores and fetches\n   216\tbytes, while verification recomputes `image-hash`.\n   217\t\n   218\t## 3. Lowering and scope\n   219\t\n   220\tLowering is two stages, both pure stream-to-stream functions:\n   221\t\n   222\t    (resolve named-datoms)          -> resolved tuples + binder side table\n   223\t    (lower-stack resolved-tuples)   -> canonical stack image + pc side table\n   224\t\n   225\t`resolve` is the de Bruijn encoding of the named semantic tuples. It is the\n   226\tshared upstream of the stack projection specified here and the register\n   227\tprojection specified in `yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`; neither projection\n   228\tis derived from the other, matching the parallel-projection topology in\n   229\t`docs/agents/architecture.md`. `resolve` receives complete `:yin/*` named\n   230\tdatoms and reuses only the public `yin.vm.debruijn/resolve-name` helper.\n   231\tThat helper compares names exactly, including rightmost-wins duplicate\n   232\tparameters, and does not canonicalize values. Projection-only helpers such\n   233\tas `index-frame`, `build-node`, and `project-node` are not reused.\n   234\tUnexpanded-macro validation remains the named front end's responsibility.\n   235\tThe projection is therefore kept, not removed: if it is ever retired, this\n   236\tpublic helper must first move into the resolver's namespace.\n   237\t\n   238\t### 3.1 Resolved tuples\n   239\t\n   240\tThe resolved tuples are the named datoms with exactly two changes, the same\n   241\ttwo changes the B1 opcode table makes to the named instruction table. Every\n   242\tother datom is carried unchanged: entity ids, `:yin/type`, operands, exact\n   243\tscalar values, exact free names, `:yin/tail?`, and node structure. Entity\n   244\tids are preserved, so a resolved node's provenance is its own id and no\n   245\tseparate provenance table exists at this level.\n   246\t\n   247\t1. A `:variable` node loses its `:yin/name` datom and gains either\n   248\t   `:yin.resolved/depth` and `:yin.resolved/position` (a bound reference,\n   249\t   from `resolve-name`'s `{:bound [depth position]}`) or `:yin.resolved/free`\n   250\t   carrying the exact symbol (from `{:free name}`).\n   251\t2. A `:lambda` node loses its `:yin/params` datom and gains\n   252\t   `:yin.resolved/arity`, the parameter count.\n   253\t\n   254\tThe attribute namespace is `:yin.resolved/*`, not `:yin.debruijn/*`, which\n   255\tthe dormant projection owns. The binder side table is `{lambda-entity\n   256\t[param-symbol ...]}`: the exact parameter vector of every lambda, keyed by\n   257\tits entity. No name is recorded per variable occurrence; a bound occurrence's\n   258\toriginal spelling is the owning lambda's parameter at the resolved position,\n   259\twhich `resolve-name`'s contract guarantees is exact.\n   260\t\n   261\tThe walk is a tree walk in the named linearizer's order: operator then\n   262\toperands left to right, `if` test then arms, lambda bodies with the lambda's\n   263\tparameter vector pushed innermost-first on the resolution stack for the\n   264\tbody's extent. Depth 0 is the innermost enclosing lambda. This stack is\n   265\tthe resolver's only state; the runtime frame vector's outermost-first order\n   266\tis B3's, and the conversion between them is explicit there.\n   267\t\n   268\t`resolve` refuses with a qualified diagnostic, before any lowerer runs, a\n   269\tprogram whose bound reference falls outside its enclosing arity chain, an\n   270\tunsupported node type, or a host value in a data operand. A hand-built\n   271\tresolved tuple set with `:yin.resolved/depth 5` and no enclosing lambda is\n   272\trejected by `resolve`'s own validator, which walks the tree once more\n   273\tagainst the arity chain.\n   274\t\n   275\tThe resolved tuples are a derived stage value. They have this shape\n   276\tcontract and namespace so that two lowerers and their tests agree on what\n   277\tthey consume, but no hash, no identity, and no sharing role: H and R are\n   278\tthe only executable identities (D9, D12), the tuples are not executable,\n   279\tand \"derive, do not persist\" forbids a persisted copy of facts the named\n   280\tdatoms already hold. They are not the second lossless view D2 rejects:\n   281\tnever stored as a source view, never served, never a fetch key. With the\n   282\tbinder side table they invert to the named datoms; that inverse is the\n   283\tresolver's test oracle only.\n   284\t\n   285\t### 3.2 Stack lowering\n   286\t\n   287\t`lower-stack` consumes resolved tuples and emits the canonical positional\n   288\t`:yin.debruijn.code/*` image (section 2) plus the pc-keyed diagnostic side\n   289\ttable the lift needs. It cannot call `yin.vm.linearize/lower`, which reads\n   290\t`:yin/name` and `:yin/params`. It therefore reproduces the named\n   291\tlinearizer's flattening itself: the same recursion in evaluation order, the\n   292\tsame `:push` after operator and after each operand, `:call` with argc and\n   293\tthe copied `:yin/tail?`, `if` as `branch-false`, `jump`, and two labels,\n   294\t`:halt` after the root, lambda bodies out of line in discovery order each\n   295\tending in `:return`, and labels resolved to pcs in a second pass. A\n   296\t`:variable` emits `[:load-bound depth position]` or `[:load-free name]`\n   297\tstraight from its resolved attributes; a `:lambda` emits `[:closure arity\n   298\tbody-pc]`. Every other node emits the carried mnemonic unchanged.\n   299\t\n   300\tLayout equality with the named linearizer no longer holds by construction.\n   301\tIt is a required structural-comparison test: for every corpus program the\n   302\tcanonical vector of `(lower x)` and `(lower-stack (resolve x))` have the\n   303\tsame length and the same mnemonic at every pc, and differ only at `:var`\n   304\tversus `:load-bound`/`:load-free` operands and at `:closure`'s parameter\n   305\tversus arity operand. A layout change in `yin.vm.linearize` must fail this\n   306\ttest rather than silently fork H.\n   307\t\n   308\t`lower-stack` copies `:yin/tail?` exactly as it was resolved. It does not\n   309\tinfer tail position from syntax. Only `:application` has a tail operand in\n   310\tthe existing instruction table; `:dao.stream.apply/call` lowers to\n   311\t`:ffi-call` and has no tail operand.\n   312\t\n   313\tThe pc side table is `{pc {:kind :closure, :params [sym ...], :source e}}`\n   314\tfor every `:closure` pc, read from the binder side table by lambda entity,\n   315\tand `{pc {:kind :var, :source e}}` for every load pc. `adapt` is the\n   316\tcomposition `lower-stack` after `resolve` and is the one public entry that\n   317\ttakes named datoms to an image.\n   318\t\n   319\tThis removes the fused design's vector-level scope reconstruction:\n   320\t`closure-body-ranges`, `layout-conforms?`, `body-owner`, `chain-of`, and\n   321\tthe vector-level `resolve-var` and `rewrite`. That machinery existed only\n   322\tbecause resolution was placed after linearization and had to recover body\n   323\tscopes from an already flattened vector. Scope is now known during the tree\n   324\twalk, and B1's validator remains the independent vector-level check.\n   325\t\n   326\tThe executable image may carry a debug node hash and named source reference.\n   327\tThose fields are always diagnostic metadata outside code identity. They replace\n   328\tno named source map.\n   329\t\n   330\tScope validation is mandatory in both places where an image can enter:\n   331\t\n   332\t1. `resolve` validates nonnegative depth and position against the enclosing\n   333\t   arity chain at resolution time, on the tree.\n   334\t2. B1's image validator repeats the check by walking each body's declared\n   335\t   arity and enclosing-body chain. A shape-valid hand-built image with\n   336\t   `[:load-bound [5 0]]` is rejected before execution.\n   337\t\n   338\tNo projected reader participates in this path; only the B1 image validator\n   339\tadmits executable images.\n   340\t\n   341\t## 4. VM state and execution\n   342\t\n   343\tThe sibling VM uses explicit state:\n   344\t\n   345\t    {:segment code-image\n   346\t     :pc pc\n   347\t     :frames [frame ...]\n   348\t     :free-env initial-name-map\n   349\t     :stack operand-vector\n   350\t     :continuation continuation-data\n   351\t     :store store\n   352\t     :status status}\n   353\t\n   354\tFrames are positional vectors ordered outermost to innermost; frame zero for a\n   355\tbound reference is the innermost frame, read from the end of the vector. A\n   356\tclosure captures the persistent frame stack and its body reference. Calls use\n   357\tthe positional equivalent of `bind-params`: `(vec (take arity (concat args\n   358\t(repeat nil))))`. Missing arguments are nil-filled and extras are dropped.\n   359\tThe existing named `bind-params` remains name-keyed and unchanged.\n   360\t\n   361\t`:load-free` uses the existing `resolve-var` order with `free-env`, store,\n   362\tprimitives, and module registry. Positional locals never fall through to a\n   363\tstore key. `:macro?` is retained only in named datoms; runtime closure\n   364\tapplication ignores it exactly as the named runtime does after macro expansion.\n   365\t\n   366\tThe VM is a new `yin.vm.debruijn.stack` namespace. It may implement existing VM\n   367\tprotocols without adding methods, and it may reuse data-only engine helpers\n   368\tfor name resolution, primitive descriptions, stream descriptors, store\n   369\toperations, gensym, and continuation records. It reimplements any helper\n   370\tthat serializes the named register layout, including private response wait\n   371\tentries and named telemetry snapshots. No existing protocol or VM semantics\n   372\tchange.\n   373\t\n   374\tIt does not implement `vm/IVMState/environment` until a frame-to-named lift is\n   375\tdefined. If that method is later implemented, it returns the lifted named\n   376\tenvironment, not the positional frame vector.\n   377\t\n   378\tPark and resume carry the frame stack, free environment, operand stack, code\n   379\tidentity, continuation chain, store view, parked records, gensym counter,\n   380\tprimitives, modules, code aliases, and the semantic VM's other non-environment\n   381\tregisters as explicit data. B4 supplies a frame-aware completion adapter or a\n   382\tlift through the descriptor morphism before exporting a continuation. UCF\n   383\tremains proposed and deferred, so heterogeneous continuation transport is not\n   384\tyet promised.\n   385\t\n   386\t### 4.1 Engine seam (B4)\n   387\t\n   388\t`yin.vm.engine` is the shared scheduler for every Yin VM, and the contract\n   389\tbetween it and a VM is stated once in `yin.vm.engine.md`: the engine owns a\n   390\tclosed set of bookkeeping keys on the state map and a closed set of keys on\n   540\t\n   541\t### B3: de Bruijn VM kernel\n   542\t\n   543\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc\n   544\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_test.cljc\n   545\t    Existing edits: none\n   546\t    Must not change: semantic VM, engine, IVM protocols, named environment,\n   547\t    merged projection namespace\n   548\t\n   549\tImplement frames, closures, loads, calls, returns, branches, literals, and\n   550\tstore operations. Completion requires pure-program parity using B0's\n   551\tnormalizer and fresh initial environments, plus the informational section 1\n   552\tbenchmark report, not an acceptance condition.\n   553\t\n   554\t### B4: effects and continuations\n   555\t\n   556\t    Existing source: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc\n   557\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_effects_test.cljc\n   558\t    Existing edits: src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc (additive only:\n   559\t      scheduler-round; resume-from-run-queue calls restore-fn with three\n   560\t      arguments), src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc (additive only:\n   561\t      response-wait-entry), test/yin/vm/engine_test.cljc (fake restore\n   562\t      arity; tests for the two additions)\n   563\t    Must not change: dao.stream protocols, lease, waitset, named effect rules,\n   564\t    merged projection namespace, yin.vm.semantic, yin.vm.ast_walker (B4's\n   565\t    parity oracles; their migration to the engine additions is a separate\n   566\t    commit), any existing engine outcome or entry key\n   567\t\n   568\tImplement stream operations, primitives, FFI, gensym, current-continuation,\n   569\tpark, and resume against the engine seam in section 4.1 and\n   570\t`yin.vm.engine.md`. The engine edits are exactly the three listed in that\n   571\tdocument's section 7; B4 adds no other engine function and hand-writes no\n   572\tscheduler round, run-queue wrapper, or two-arity restore shim. Completion\n   573\trequires parity for values, errors, effects, stores, stream outcomes, and\n   574\tblocked states. Cross-program park/resume tests must use only\n   575\tinitial-environment or store-resolved free names until the named-VM\n   576\tenvironment leak is fixed. Before a continuation is exported, B4 must either\n   577\tlift frames through the descriptor morphism or run the frame-aware\n   578\tcompletion adapter so `:yin.k/requires` is not under-approximated.\n   579\t\n   580\t### B5: differential integration\n   581\t\n   582\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_parity_test.cljc\n   583\t    Existing edits: none\n   584\t    Must not change: named storage and the existing linearizer pipeline\n   585\t\n   586\tCompare `ast->datoms` -> B2 -> de Bruijn VM against\n   587\t`ast->datoms` -> `linearize/lower` -> semantic VM. Use the actual parity and\n   588\tcontent/completion corpora, not helper-only tests as execution fixtures.\n   589\tCompletion requires programs differing only in binder names to produce the\n   590\tsame H on every host lane for the common scalar domain. Programs differing in\n   591\texact scalar spelling, front-end tail flags, or free names must produce\n   592\tdifferent H values. An image sent over a `dao.stream` from one host lane must\n   593\tload, validate, and execute on another with the same normalized result as\n   594\tlocal execution. B5 uses committed golden bytes for cross-runtime identity;\n   595\tthe real cross-process stream transfer is a B6 acceptance test. All three host\n   596\tlanes must agree under the normalizer.\n   597\t\n   598\t### B6: committed closed-image stream linker\n   599\t\n   600\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc\n   601\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_responder.cljc\n   602\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc\n   603\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_responder_test.cljc\n   604\t    Existing edits: none\n   605\t    Must not change: merged projection namespace, image-hash, dao.stream,\n   606\t    named VM semantics\n   607\t\n   608\tImplement fetch-by-H for closed images. B6 tests use an explicit fetch; the\n   609\t`:call-hash` instruction is emitted only by the later dependency linker. A\n   610\thost emits a REQUEST value carrying H, and a responder process holds an\n   611\texplicit value mapping H to image bytes or queries an H-to-address datom chosen\n   612\tby its composition. The response carries canonical wire bytes or a qualified\n   613\tabsence/unsupported outcome. The receiver hashes those bytes before decoding,\n   614\tchecks H and the descriptor, runs the closure check, validates, and loads only\n   615\tverified bytes. Every free name must resolve through primitives or modules and\n   616\tmust not be shadowed by free-env or store; failures are `:unresolved-free` or\n   617\t`:shadowed-free`. Completion requires JVM to Dart stream transfer, where the\n   618\treceiver initially knows only H, mismatch and unsupported refusal, absence\n   619\tevents, and equal normalized results using the existing semantic VM via lift.\n   620\t\n   621\t### B7: dependency closure linker\n   622\t\n   623\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc\n   624\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_dependency_test.cljc\n   625\t    Existing edits: none\n   626\t    Must not change: merged projection namespace or B6 closed-image semantics\n   627\t\n   628\tAdd value/stream name environments, hash-of-unit dependency closure, and\n   629\tstrongly connected component manifests. Completion requires cycle-safe\n   630\tordering, explicit authority and provenance, and stream-defined retry,\n   631\ttimeout, and permanent-absence outcomes.\n   632\t\n   633\t## 7. Test matrix and non-goals\n   634\t\n   635\tThe matrix includes exact scalar spelling, names, tail flags,\n   636\tprovenance, scope, duplicate parameters, nil fill, extras dropped, control\n   637\tflow, streams, effects, continuations, macros, malformed images, and all\n   638\texisting parity/content/completion execution fixtures. It also includes\n   639\talpha-equivalent binder renames with equal image hashes, exact-spelling and\n   640\ttail-flag differences with distinct hashes, free-name changes, stream transfer,\n   641\tand cross-host code-image bytes.\n   642\t\n   643\tNon-goals are equality saturation, a new JIT, global distribution beyond B6\n   644\tand B7, and any change to the merged dormant projection or its fingerprint.\n   645\t\n   646\t### 7.1 Prior art: Unison's runtime\n   647\t\n   648\tUnison's published runtime documentation describes let-rec minimization,\n   649\tlambda lifting, ANF, an IR with De Bruijn indices and stack positions, and\n   650\tdecompilation. The supplied source listing is reported to contain runtime\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md | sed -n '500,545p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '1,190p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '190,380p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md | sed -n '500,545p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '1,190p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '190,380p'\"","aggregated_output":"   500\t\n   501\t### B2: resolver and stack lowerer\n   502\t\n   503\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_resolve.cljc\n   504\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_resolve_test.cljc\n   505\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc\n   506\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize_test.cljc\n   507\t    Existing edits: none\n   508\t    Must not change: yin.vm.linearize and named lowering semantics\n   509\t\n   510\tB2 is two namespaces (section 3). `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve` owns `resolve`:\n   511\tnamed datoms to resolved tuples plus binder side table, its scope validator,\n   512\tand the inverse `unresolve` used as a test oracle. It is shared with the\n   513\tregister design and depends on nothing in this design below section 3.1.\n   514\t`yin.vm.debruijn-linearize` owns `lower-stack`, `adapt`, `lift`, and the\n   515\tpublic `named-canonical-vector` helper the structural comparison uses. The\n   516\tfused implementation's `closure-body-ranges`, `layout-conforms?`,\n   517\t`body-owner`, `chain-of`, vector-level `resolve-var`, `rewrite`, and the\n   518\tduplicate vector-level `image-scope-defect` are removed, not moved: scope\n   519\tis known on the tree, and B1's validator is the vector-level check.\n   520\t\n   521\tCompletion requires, for the resolver: deterministic output on every host,\n   522\tevery node type resolved or refused with a named diagnostic, exact free\n   523\tnames and scalars, the duplicate-parameter fixture `(fn [x x] x)` resolving\n   524\tto `[0 1]`, out-of-range hand-built resolved tuples refused, and\n   525\t`unresolve(resolve x, side-table) = x` over the corpus.\n   526\t\n   527\tFor the stack lowerer: deterministic output, every node and opcode, the\n   528\tsection 3.2 structural opcode-by-opcode comparison with `lower` differing\n   529\tonly at variable and closure operands, image encode/validate/load round\n   530\ttrips, and `lift(adapt x, side-table) = canonical-vector(lower x)`. The lift\n   531\tis a function of the image plus its pc side table. With the original binder\n   532\tnames in the side table, equality holds; with synthesized names, the lifted\n   533\tresult is alpha-equivalent to `lower x`, including `(fn [x x] x)`.\n   534\tSynthesized names must be fresh against the image's free-name set. A\n   535\tsupplied side table is accepted only when `adapt` of its lift returns the\n   536\toriginal image. Every image B2 emits must be accepted by B1's validator, and\n   537\tevery hand-built out-of-range image must be rejected by B1's validator. The\n   538\tgolden image bytes and H values frozen in B1 must be reproduced unchanged\n   539\tby `adapt`; the refactor changes derivation, not bytes.\n   540\t\n   541\t### B3: de Bruijn VM kernel\n   542\t\n   543\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc\n   544\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_test.cljc\n   545\t    Existing edits: none\n     1\t# yin.vm de Bruijn register VM\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design, revised; not implemented\n     4\t\n     5\tThis document specifies an optional register execution path that is a peer\n     6\tof the committed de Bruijn stack path. Both are projections of the same\n     7\tde Bruijn encoding of the named semantic tuples; neither is derived from the\n     8\tother. It does not replace the named datoms, the stack image, the stack VM,\n     9\tor the dormant projection. The first deliverable is a pure resolved-tuples-\n    10\tto-register lowerer and validator. A register VM kernel is a later gated\n    11\tphase, not an assumption of this design.\n    12\t\n    13\t## 1. Objective and invariants\n    14\t\n    15\tThe objective is a deterministic register image derived from the de Bruijn\n    16\tencoding of the named `:yin/*` semantic tuples, at the same level as the\n    17\t`:yin.debruijn.code/*` stack image. The register lowerer consumes the\n    18\tresolved tuples and emits a register image; it never reads, rewrites, or\n    19\tdepends on the stack image. The stack image remains independently\n    20\taddressable by its existing H, and the register image is independently\n    21\taddressable by its own R.\n    22\t\n    23\tThis follows the project's established compilation topology\n    24\t(`docs/agents/architecture.md`, \"COMPILATION AS STREAM PROCESSING\"): one\n    25\tupstream stream feeds several parallel projections, and no projection is\n    26\tcompiled from another. That pattern is applied here one level down, at the\n    27\tde Bruijn layer.\n    28\t\n    29\tThis proposal is worth considering on its own merits because it targets an\n    30\talpha-invariant, content-addressed encoding rather than reintroducing a VM\n    31\tdirectly beside the AST walker. It is still at risk of becoming an\n    32\tunnecessary second evaluator. Therefore the lowerer and format may ship\n    33\twithout a kernel, and a kernel is allowed only after the benchmark gate in\n    34\tR3 reports a material benefit over the committed stack VM.\n    35\t\n    36\tThe following invariants apply:\n    37\t\n    38\t1. A register image is derived data, derived from the resolved tuples. The\n    39\t   stack image is never consumed, rewritten, or invalidated by the register\n    40\t   path, and neither image is derived from the other.\n    41\t2. `dao.stream` is the stage boundary. Every lowering is a pure stream-to-\n    42\t   stream function; no stage mutates an input stream or retains a callback.\n    43\t3. H and R are peer format identities, not competing semantic identities.\n    44\t   H identifies the exact stack bytes; R identifies the exact register bytes\n    45\t   that a register-capable host can run. Neither preimage mentions the other.\n    46\t4. Equal R implies equal register bytes and descriptor contract. It does not\n    47\t   imply that a stack VM can execute those bytes.\n    48\t5. Register execution must agree with the named semantic VM, and therefore\n    49\t   with the stack VM, under the B0 normalizer: results, errors, store\n    50\t   effects, stream outcomes, tail calls, and lexical resolution.\n    51\t6. All register allocation choices are data-derived and deterministic across\n    52\t   runs and hosts.\n    53\t\n    54\tThe existing stack design's D1-D16 decisions remain authoritative. The\n    55\tregister path adds no projection fingerprint and no global registry. It\n    56\tproposes one change to the stack design's B2 file box, recorded in section\n    57\t2.2 and deferred to the owner; everything else in `yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`\n    58\tis unchanged.\n    59\t\n    60\t### 1.1 Identity and sharing\n    61\t\n    62\tThe register descriptor is `:yin.debruijn.register/*`. Its `register-hash`\n    63\tfunction, R, hashes the register descriptor hash and the canonical\n    64\tpositional register vector, exactly as B1's `image-hash` hashes the stack\n    65\tdescriptor hash and the canonical stack vector. R is the only identity used\n    66\tto fetch, verify, cache, or execute register bytes. A linker request must\n    67\tname both the format and R, for example\n    68\t`{:format :yin.debruijn.register :hash R}`.\n    69\t\n    70\tR is alpha-invariant for binder renames for the same reason H is: the\n    71\tregister vector carries `:load-bound` depth and position operands and\n    72\t`:closure` arities, and binder names live in a diagnostic side table outside\n    73\tthe hash. Equal R therefore implies agreement on exact scalar spelling, free\n    74\tname operands, and front-end tail flags, as equal H does. Two source\n    75\tprograms that share H share R, and the converse holds, because both are\n    76\tpure functions of the same resolved tuples; this is a testable law, not a\n    77\thashed relation.\n    78\t\n    79\tThis is not a second semantic answer to alpha-equivalence. A register\n    80\tlowerer cannot repair differences in exact scalar spelling, free names, or\n    81\tfront-end tail flags; those differences already make the resolved tuples,\n    82\tand so both H and R, differ.\n    83\t\n    84\tThe pairing of an R with the H of the same named root is a composition\n    85\tfact, carried beside a request or recorded as a datom by whoever publishes\n    86\tboth, never inside either preimage. A receiver verifies a fetched register\n    87\timage with R alone and never needs to have seen the stack image.\n    88\t\n    89\t## 2. Compilation topology and stream contract\n    90\t\n    91\tThe register projection is a peer of the stack projection. Both branch from\n    92\tthe resolved tuples, the de Bruijn encoding of the named AST datoms:\n    93\t\n    94\t    Source -> Yang -> named AST datoms (:yin/*)\n    95\t                         |\\\n    96\t                         | \\-> named semantic image (:yin.code/*)\n    97\t                         |\n    98\t                         +----> resolved tuples (de Bruijn AST, no H)\n    99\t                                   |\\\n   100\t                                   | \\-> stack image (H)      B1 shape, B2\n   101\t                                   |\n   102\t                                   +---> register image (R)   R1\n   103\t\n   104\tThe register edge is not chained after the stack image. The named stream,\n   105\tthe resolved tuples, the stack image, and the register image coexist and\n   106\tremain independently queryable or persistable, per the pipeline invariant\n   107\tthat no stage destroys its input.\n   108\t\n   109\tThe stages have the pure shapes:\n   110\t\n   111\t    (resolve named-datom-stream)      -> resolved-tuple-stream\n   112\t    (lower-stack resolved-stream)     -> stack-image-stream     (H)\n   113\t    (lower-register resolved-stream)  -> register-image-stream  (R)\n   114\t\n   115\tEach register output item carries a register descriptor reference, the\n   116\tcanonical register vector, R, and a diagnostic side table. The implementation\n   117\tmay build indexes while evaluating one bounded item, but those indexes are\n   118\tlocal function data and are not written back into any input.\n   119\t\n   120\tThe register stage refuses malformed resolved tuples, unsupported node\n   121\ttypes, or out-of-range bound operands with qualified data diagnostics. It\n   122\tdoes not silently fall back inside the lowerer. A composition may route the\n   123\tsame named root to the stack path after receiving that outcome.\n   124\t\n   125\t### 2.1 The shared artifact: resolved tuples\n   126\t\n   127\tThe resolved tuples are the named `:yin/*` datoms with exactly two changes,\n   128\tthe same two changes B1's opcode table makes to the named instruction table:\n   129\t\n   130\t1. Every `:variable` node is resolved by the public\n   131\t   `yin.vm.debruijn/resolve-name` against the innermost-first stack of\n   132\t   enclosing parameter vectors, to either a bound reference `[depth\n   133\t   position]` or a free reference carrying the exact symbol.\n   134\t2. Every `:lambda` node carries its arity instead of its parameter vector.\n   135\t\n   136\tEverything else is carried exactly: scalar values with their exact spelling,\n   137\tfree names as supplied, `:yin/tail?` as the front end wrote it, node\n   138\tstructure, and evaluation order. Binder names and provenance go to a\n   139\tdiagnostic side table keyed by node, as B2's side table is keyed by pc.\n   140\tNothing is canonicalized: this is not the dormant merged projection, which\n   141\tcanonicalizes scalars, NFC-folds names, and drops tail flags, and which the\n   142\tstack design's architecture B rejected as a lowering input for those reasons.\n   143\t\n   144\tThe resolver is the tree-level home of name resolution. It validates each\n   145\tbound reference against the enclosing arity chain, the same nonnegative-\n   146\tdepth-and-position rule B2 checks on the vector, and refuses a program whose\n   147\tresolution fails before either lowerer runs. It reuses only\n   148\t`resolve-name`; projection-only helpers are not reused, and the resolver\n   149\tinherits the stack design's retirement condition for that helper.\n   150\t\n   151\tThe resolved tuples have a shape contract and a namespace so that two\n   152\tlowerers and their tests can agree on what they consume. They have no hash,\n   153\tno identity, and no sharing role:\n   154\t\n   155\t- Invariant I shares executable code. The resolved tuples are not executable\n   156\t  by any VM in this project; the executable artifacts are the stack image\n   157\t  and the register image, identified by H and R.\n   158\t- D9 and D12 make H the only identity on the stack sharing path, computed by\n   159\t  one function over the descriptor hash and the canonical vector. R mirrors\n   160\t  that rule for the register path. A hash of the resolved tuples would be a\n   161\t  third identity that no request, response, verification, or cache needs.\n   162\t- \"Derive, do not persist\" (`datom.world.md`): the resolved tuples are a\n   163\t  pure function of the named datoms and `resolve-name`. Persisting them or\n   164\t  giving them an identity would duplicate authority over facts the named\n   165\t  datoms already hold. They are a stage value: computed, consumed, and\n   166\t  discardable. A composition that chooses to retain the stream may do so,\n   167\t  as it may retain any intermediate, but nothing downstream keys on it.\n   168\t- D2 and section 7.3 of the stack design forbid a second lossless record\n   169\t  DAG. The resolved tuples are not one: they are never stored as a source\n   170\t  view, never served, and never a fetch key. With the side table they are\n   171\t  invertible to the named datoms, but that property is used only by tests.\n   172\t\n   173\t### 2.2 Relation to B2 as implemented\n   174\t\n   175\tB2 today produces the stack image in one fused pass: it runs the named\n   176\tlinearizer `yin.vm.linearize/lower`, converts the result to the canonical\n   177\tnamed vector, reconstructs body scopes from that vector, and rewrites `:var`\n   178\tto `:load-bound` or `:load-free` and `:closure` parameters to arity. Its de\n   179\tBruijn addressing therefore happens after stack-shape assembly, over an\n   180\talready push-and-argc shaped vector. There is no point inside B2 at which an\n   181\texecution-model-agnostic de Bruijn form exists.\n   182\t\n   183\tThe peer topology above does not require that file box to change in order\n   184\tto ship R0-R2. B2's fused pass and the two-stage path `lower-stack` after\n   185\t`resolve` are extensionally equal: the named linearizer's walk order,\n   186\toccurrence expansion, and body layout are what `lower-stack` reproduces,\n   187\tand resolution commutes with that walk because it reads only the enclosing\n   188\tparameter stack. The register design therefore binds the two by a tested\n   189\tlaw rather than by construction:\n   190\t\n   190\t\n   191\t    addresses(B2 stack image) = addresses(register image)\n   192\t                              = addresses(resolved tuples)\n   193\t\n   194\twhere `addresses` is the sequence of resolved variable references, bound\n   195\t`[depth position]` or free name, in evaluation order: the main sequence\n   196\tfirst, then each out-of-line body in discovery order. The register lowerer\n   197\tpins that same body layout order so the three sequences align position by\n   198\tposition. This law is an R0 fixture over the whole B0 parity corpus.\n   199\t\n   200\tRecommended, and deferred to the owner because it edits the stack design's\n   201\tsection 3 and B2 box: refactor B2, while it is still uncommitted, into\n   202\t`resolve` followed by a stack linearization of the resolved tuples. That\n   203\tremoves B2's vector-level scope reconstruction (`closure-body-ranges`,\n   204\t`layout-conforms?`, `body-owner`, `chain-of`), which exists only because\n   205\tresolution was placed after linearization, and makes the stack image\n   206\tliterally a peer projection rather than an equivalent one. The cost is a\n   207\tcopy of the named linearizer's flattening walk in the stack lowerer, since\n   208\t`lower` cannot consume resolved tuples, and the loss of by-construction\n   209\tlayout equality with `lower`, which becomes the existing structural-\n   210\tcomparison test instead. Every golden H is unchanged either way, because the\n   211\temitted bytes are identical; the address law and B1's golden fixtures prove\n   212\tit. If the owner declines, B2 stays as is and the address law is the only\n   213\tcoupling.\n   214\t\n   215\t## 3. Register dimension\n   216\t\n   217\tThe descriptor declares:\n   218\t\n   219\t    +----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   220\t    | Field                | Rule                                          |\n   221\t    +----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   222\t    | Namespace            | :yin.debruijn.register/*                      |\n   223\t    | Contract version     | Explicit integer, included in descriptor H    |\n   224\t    | Source               | Resolved tuples (section 2.1)                 |\n   225\t    | Register image       | Body ranges plus positional instructions      |\n   226\t    | Scalar encoding      | Reuse B1 scalar bytes, never projection NFC   |\n   227\t    | Lexical addressing   | :load-bound depth/position remains explicit   |\n   228\t    | Free addressing      | :load-free name remains exact                 |\n   229\t    | Validation           | Shape, targets, body scope, register bounds   |\n   230\t    | Lift                 | Register image to named semantics             |\n   231\t    +----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   232\t\n   233\tThe register descriptor has its own validator because register operands and\n   234\tbody register counts are new facts. It reuses B1's public data rules and\n   235\tscalar encoder only through an explicit dependency; it does not call private\n   236\tprojection helpers. The descriptor version is incremented whenever opcode\n   237\tshape, allocation, scalar framing, or control-flow rules change.\n   238\t\n   239\tThe lowerer's R formula is:\n   240\t\n   241\t    R = sha256(register-descriptor-hash || canonical-register-vector)\n   242\t\n   243\tBoth components are canonical bytes. Received register bytes are hashed\n   244\tbefore decoding, as in B1's wire rule. A receiver verifies R, checks the\n   245\tdescriptor, runs the receiver closure check over `:load-free` operands\n   246\texactly as D11 and D15 define it for stack images, validates, and only then\n   247\texecutes. No relation to a stack H is embedded or checked.\n   248\t\n   249\tThe lift goes from the register image to the named `:yin.code/*` image with\n   250\tdiagnostic or synthesized binder names, so that B6's rule \"a fetched image\n   251\tmay be executed by the semantic VM after lifting\" holds for register images\n   252\ttoo. It is a function of the register image plus its side table.\n   253\t\n   254\t## 4. Resolved-tuples-to-register lowering\n   255\t\n   256\t### 4.1 Input and validation\n   257\t\n   258\tThe lowerer accepts only resolved tuples from the section 2.1 resolver and\n   259\ttheir side table. It does not accept named datoms directly, does not rerun\n   260\tname resolution, and does not accept a stack image. B2 remains the only\n   261\tproducer of `:yin.debruijn.code/*` stack images; this lowerer is the only\n   262\tproducer of `:yin.debruijn.register/*` register images.\n   263\t\n   264\tBecause the input is a tree, there is no control-flow graph to recover and\n   265\tno abstract operand stack to simulate: every intermediate value is an\n   266\texpression whose extent is known from the tree. The walk is the named\n   267\tlinearizer's order, operator then operands left to right, lambda bodies out\n   268\tof line in discovery order, `if` evaluating one arm. Every body has one\n   269\tenclosing lexical chain by construction, and the resolver has already\n   270\tvalidated every bound reference against it. The lowerer's own checks are\n   271\tregister bounds, body ranges, and target bounds, applied to its output.\n   272\t\n   273\t### 4.2 Register classes\n   274\t\n   275\tEach body has two disjoint register banks:\n   276\t\n   277\t    L0 ... L(n-1)     current body's parameters\n   278\t    T0 ... T(k-1)     expression temporaries\n   279\t\n   280\t`n` is the body's declared arity. `:load-bound rd depth position` reads the\n   281\texisting lexical frame chain and writes `rd`; it does not assign an outer\n   282\tframe's slot a register number in the current body. Thus frame 0 remains the\n   283\tinnermost frame and the runtime frame vector remains outermost-first, exactly\n   284\tas in B3. `:load-free rd name` preserves the existing resolution order.\n   285\t\n   286\tThe allocator gives every intermediate expression value a virtual temporary\n   287\tin evaluation order, then performs deterministic linear-scan allocation\n   288\twithin each body. Locals are never coalesced with temporaries. The physical\n   289\tregister count is the number of local registers plus the maximum\n   290\tsimultaneously live temporary registers. If a target requires a bounded\n   291\tregister file, deterministic spill slots are allocated after the last\n   292\tregister using the same order; spilling is a later kernel concern and is not\n   293\timplicit in R1.\n   294\t\n   295\t### 4.3 Determinism\n   296\t\n   297\tLowering from a tree removes the join-order risk a stack-image compiler\n   298\twould have: there are no predecessor traversals to order, and both arms of\n   299\tan `if` write the expression's one destination register. The remaining\n   300\trisks are hash-map iteration, liveness tie breaks, and spill choices.\n   301\t\n   302\tThe lowerer closes these risks by using evaluation order for virtual-value\n   303\tdefinition, lowest available register, and lowest virtual-id tie breaks.\n   304\tAny moves that a target convention requires are sorted by destination\n   305\tregister and emitted in a fixed cycle-breaking order. No host map iteration\n   306\torder may affect an emitted vector. R1 pins golden register vectors and R\n   307\tvalues for a corpus on JVM, CLJS, and CLJD.\n   308\t\n   309\t### 4.4 Instruction mapping\n   310\t\n   311\tThe register instruction set is a positional form of the B1 table:\n   312\t\n   313\t    :const        [op rd value]\n   314\t    :load-bound   [op rd depth position]\n   315\t    :load-free    [op rd name]\n   316\t    :closure      [op rd arity body-pc]\n   317\t    :move         [op rd rs]\n   318\t    :call         [op rd fn-reg arg-regs tail?]\n   319\t    :branch-false [op cond-reg target]\n   320\t    :jump         [op target]\n   321\t    :return       [op value-reg]\n   322\t    :halt         [op value-reg]\n   323\t    :store-get    [op rd key]\n   324\t    :store-put    [op key value-reg]\n   325\t\n   326\tThere is no `:push`: operands are named by register, so the stack path's\n   327\tpush-before-each-operand convention never arises. `:call` differs most from\n   328\tits stack form: instead of an argc over an operand stack it names an\n   329\texplicit function register, an ordered argument-register vector, a\n   330\tdestination register, and the tail flag copied from `:yin/tail?`.\n   331\t`:branch-false`, `:return`, and `:halt` name registers instead of reading an\n   332\toperand stack. Constants, lexical loads, free loads, closures, jumps, and\n   333\tstore operations retain their semantic operands while gaining explicit\n   334\tdestinations where needed.\n   335\t\n   336\tStream, gensym, FFI, park, and resume instructions use the same explicit\n   337\tdestination and source-register convention in R2. Their effect descriptors\n   338\tremain stream values; no callback or hidden scheduler is introduced.\n   339\t\n   340\t## 5. Execution boundary\n   341\t\n   342\tR1 may produce and validate register bytecode without a register kernel. This\n   343\tis the recommended first milestone and is analogous to B1's standalone\n   344\tdimension, but the register format is not a promise that a second evaluator\n   345\twill exist.\n   346\t\n   347\tIf the benchmark gate passes, R3 may add\n   348\t`yin.vm.debruijn.register` as a sibling kernel. It owns explicit state\n   349\t`{:image :pc :registers :frames :free-env :continuation :store :status\n   350\t:primitives :modules}` and uses the same frame, free-name, store, stream, and\n   351\tcontinuation contracts as B3, including the engine seam B4 implements\n   352\t(`yin.vm.engine.md`: one restore function, per-site park-entry builders, the\n   353\tengine-owned key sets). It adds no `IVM` or `IVMState` methods.\n   354\t\n   355\tThe register kernel must execute only validator-approved images. It must\n   356\tpreserve B3's frame direction, closure capture, nil-fill and extra-argument\n   357\trules, and B0 normalization. Register continuations are not interchangeable\n   358\twith stack continuations unless an explicit lift is provided.\n   359\t\n   360\t## 6. Implementation phases\n   361\t\n   362\tEach phase has a bounded file box, must-not-change list, completion criteria,\n   363\tand JVM, CLJS, CLJD, kondo, and cljstyle verification.\n   364\t\n   365\t### R0: contract and corpus\n   366\t\n   367\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_contract_test.cljc\n   368\t    Existing edits: none\n   369\t    Must not change: B0-B3, named datoms, stack image, resolver contract,\n   370\t    projection namespace\n   371\t\n   372\tThe resolver (`yin.vm.debruijn-resolve`: resolved tuples, side table,\n   373\tscope validation, the inverse used by tests) is now B2's own file box, per\n   374\tthe stack design's B2 phase box (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` section 3.1) --\n   375\tnot built here. R0 depends on it and freezes the register descriptor\n   376\tversion, operand mapping, normalizer fixtures, and the named-root corpus\n   377\tagainst it. Completion requires every node type from the resolver either\n   378\tmapped or refused with a named diagnostic, the duplicate-parameter and\n   379\tfree-name fixtures from B2, the section 2.2 address law between B2's stack\n   380\timages and the resolved tuples over the B0 parity corpus, pure data\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '90,150p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; sed -n '390,455p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '40,80p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc; sed -n '285,340p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '90,150p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; sed -n '390,455p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '40,80p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc; sed -n '285,340p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"   control left, which is the halted state.\n\n   An empty continuation is stored as nil rather than [] so that\n   `engine/ready-for-ingress?`, which asks `(nil? (:k vm))`, sees a machine\n   between evaluations.\"\n  [vm seg pc val St E K]\n  (assoc vm\n         :control (when seg {:segment seg, :pc pc})\n         :value val\n         :stack (or St [])\n         :env E\n         :k (if (seq K) K nil)\n         :halted? (and (not (:blocked? vm)) (nil? seg))))\n\n\n(defn- response-wait-entry\n  \"The polling wait entry for a sent call awaiting its correlated response:\n   the machine registers {segment pc env stack k} plus the call-out reader\n   fields, in the shape `ffi/call-response-wait-entry` gives the walker.\"\n  [seg pc env stack k call-id]\n  {:segment seg, :pc pc, :env env, :stack stack, :k k,\n   :call-id call-id,\n   :reason :next,\n   :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id vm/call-out-cursor-key},\n   :stream-id vm/call-out-stream-key})\n\n\n(defn semantic-restore\n  \"Restore a wait-set or ready-queue entry into machine registers.\n\n   Two entry shapes carry more than registers:\n\n   - A `:request-sent` entry is a writer whose retained FFI request has now\n     been appended. The call starts waiting for its correlated response,\n     exactly as an immediately-sent one does, so the entry is replaced by its\n     response reader and the machine stays blocked.\n   - A `:call-id` entry is a response reader. The woken value is a response\n     envelope, so `ffi/call-result` unwraps it, checks correlation, and the\n     parked call leaves `:parked` rather than accumulating.\"\n  ([base entry] (semantic-restore base entry (:value entry)))\n  ([base entry val]\n   (if (:request-sent entry)\n     (assoc base\n            :wait-set (conj (vec (or (:wait-set base) []))\n                            (response-wait-entry (:segment entry)\n                                                 (:pc entry)\n                                                 (:env entry)\n                                                 (:stack entry)\n                                                 (:k entry)\n                                                 (:call-id entry)))\n            :control nil\n            :k nil\n            :value :yin/blocked\n            :blocked? true\n            :halted? false)\n     (let [call-id (:call-id entry)\n           base (if call-id (update base :parked dissoc call-id) base)\n           val (if call-id (ffi/call-result val call-id) val)]\n       (put-registers base\n                      (:segment entry)\n                      (:pc entry)\n   is no stream operation whose error vocabulary it belongs to.\"\n  [entry o]\n  (cond\n    (contains? waitset-diagnostics o)\n    (fail \"Wait-set entry woke with a diagnostic\" {:status o, :entry entry})\n    (= :next (:reason entry))\n    (fail \"Stream read failed\"\n          {:outcome o,\n           :stream-id (:stream-id entry),\n           :cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref entry))})\n    :else (fail \"Stream append failed\"\n                {:outcome o, :stream-id (:stream-id entry)})))\n\n\n(defn resume-from-run-queue\n  \"Pop first entry from the ready-queue, merge store-updates, and restore\n   VM-specific context.\n\n   Restoration is dispatched here rather than carried on the entry, so a\n   ready entry holds registers, ids, and values — never a closure. The\n   terminal-outcome check runs before the restore: a woken retry that ended\n   in an outcome the immediate operation raises as an error must fail the\n   same way here, not reach Yin code as a value.\"\n  [state restore-fn]\n  (let [run-queue (or (:ready-queue state) [])]\n    (when (seq run-queue)\n      (let [entry (first run-queue)\n            rest-queue (subvec run-queue 1)\n            base (assoc state\n                        :ready-queue rest-queue\n                        :store (merge (:store state) (:store-updates entry))\n                        :blocked? false\n                        :halted? false)]\n        (if-let [terminal (terminal-resume-outcome entry)]\n          (throw-terminal-resume! entry terminal)\n          (restore-fn base entry))))))\n\n\n(defn park-continuation\n  \"Add a parked continuation entry and halt the VM.\"\n  [state cont-fields]\n  (let [id-counter (or (:id-counter state) 0)\n        park-id (keyword (str \"parked-\" id-counter))\n        parked (merge {:type :parked-continuation, :id park-id} cont-fields)]\n    (-> state\n        (update :parked assoc park-id parked)\n        (assoc :value parked\n               :halted? true\n               :id-counter (inc id-counter))\n        (telemetry/emit-snapshot :park {:parked-id park-id}))))\n\n\n(defn resume-continuation\n  \"Restore state from a parked continuation.\"\n  [state parked-id resume-val restore-fn]\n  (if-let [parked (get-in state [:parked parked-id])]\n    (let [new-state (update state :parked dissoc parked-id)]\n      (-> (restore-fn new-state parked resume-val)\n          (telemetry/emit-snapshot :resume {:parked-id parked-id})))\n    (fail \"Cannot resume: parked continuation not found\"\n          {:parked-id parked-id})))\n\n\n(defn gensym\n  \"Generate a unique ID and return [id updated-state].\"\n  ([state] (gensym state \"id\"))\n\n\n(defn require-call-pair!\n  \"Fail when the VM holds no FFI pair. Callers must run this before parking:\n   an error raised after `park-continuation` strands a continuation in\n   `:parked` and consumes an id counter.\"\n  [store what]\n  (or (call-pair store)\n      (throw (ex-info \"This VM was constructed without an FFI call pair, so it cannot make a dao.stream.apply call\"\n                      {:what what}))))\n\n\n(defn call-response-wait-entry\n  \"The polling wait entry for a sent call awaiting its correlated response.\"\n  [call-id next-k env]\n  {:k {:type :dao.stream.apply/eval-call,\n       :next next-k,\n       :env env,\n       :call-id call-id},\n   :env env,\n   :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id vm/call-out-cursor-key},\n   :reason :next,\n   :stream-id vm/call-out-stream-key})\n\n\n(defn call-result\n  \"Unwrap a response envelope for the continuation that made the call.\n\n   v1 read `:dao.stream.apply/value` off a response that could only succeed.\n   A v2 response carries exactly one of `ok` or `error`, and correlation is\n   checked here rather than assumed from stream order.\"\n  [response call-id]\n  (when-not (apply2/response? response)\n    (throw (ex-info \"FFI response envelope is malformed\" {:response response})))\n  (when (and call-id (not= call-id (apply2/response-id response)))\n    (throw (ex-info \"FFI response does not correlate with this parked call\"\n                    {:call-id call-id,\n                     :response-id (apply2/response-id response)})))\n  (if-let [err (apply2/response-error response)]\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"FFI call failed: \"\n                         (:dao.stream.apply/message err))\n  {:resolve (fn [store entry]\n              (if-let [cursor-ref (:cursor-ref entry)]\n                (let [cursor-data (get store (:id cursor-ref))]\n                  {:stream (or (:stream entry)\n                               (get store (:stream-id cursor-data)))\n                   :cursor (:cursor cursor-data)})\n                {:stream (or (:stream entry) (get store (:stream-id entry)))\n                 :value (:datom entry)}))\n   :advance (fn [store entry cursor]\n              (let [cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref entry))]\n                (if cursor-id\n                  (assoc store\n                         cursor-id\n                         (assoc (get store cursor-id) :cursor cursor))\n                  store)))})\n\n\n(defn check-wait-set\n  \"Check wait-set entries against their transports.\n\n   The sweep is `dao.stream.waitset/check` — this engine was that library's\n   seed and is now its first consumer. Every entry is resolved to a live\n   handle and an opaque cursor out of the store by `waitset-resolver` and\n   polled with a synchronous `next` or `append!` — resolution happens per\n   poll, so the stored entry itself never carries a handle. There is no\n   transport-local waking to fall back from: this is the only mechanism.\n\n   Entries are polled one at a time in wait-set order, and a woken reader's\n   successor cursor is written back to the store before the next entry is\n   resolved. Waiters sharing a cursor-ref therefore read distinct values —\n   the value at the cursor wakes the first, its successor wakes the next —\n   instead of every waiter reading the value at the shared pre-poll\n   cursor. An entry that stays waiting is retained in its stored,\n   resource-id form.\n\n   Each woken result's `:status` is stamped onto its ready entry, where\n   `terminal-resume-outcome` reads it: an outcome the immediate operation\n   raises as an error fails the same way when the resume pops it — and a\n   waitset diagnostic, an entry the sweep could not poll at all, raises\n   there too, before any continuation is restored.\"\n  [state]\n  (let [wait-set (:wait-set state)]\n    (if (empty? wait-set)\n      state\n      (let [{:keys [woken store], :as result}\n            (waitset/check {:waiting wait-set} waitset-resolver (:store state))\n            v (assoc state\n                     :store store\n                     :wait-set (:waiting (:waitset result)))]\n        (update v\n                :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n                (make-woken-run-queue-entries v woken))))))\n\n\n(declare handle-effect resume-continuation)\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"I’m reviewing the three documents as one contract, with particular attention to the resolved-tuple seam and the shared scheduler’s entry lifecycle. I’ll separate defects that prevent sign-off from implementation details the phase reviews can safely own."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '1,460p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md | sed -n '220,340p;490,585p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md | sed -n '1,280p'\nnl -ba docs/agents/architecture.md | sed -n '130,183p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '1,460p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md | sed -n '220,340p;490,585p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md | sed -n '1,280p'\nnl -ba docs/agents/architecture.md | sed -n '130,183p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# yin.vm de Bruijn register VM\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design, revised; not implemented\n     4\t\n     5\tThis document specifies an optional register execution path that is a peer\n     6\tof the committed de Bruijn stack path. Both are projections of the same\n     7\tde Bruijn encoding of the named semantic tuples; neither is derived from the\n     8\tother. It does not replace the named datoms, the stack image, the stack VM,\n     9\tor the dormant projection. The first deliverable is a pure resolved-tuples-\n    10\tto-register lowerer and validator. A register VM kernel is a later gated\n    11\tphase, not an assumption of this design.\n    12\t\n    13\t## 1. Objective and invariants\n    14\t\n    15\tThe objective is a deterministic register image derived from the de Bruijn\n    16\tencoding of the named `:yin/*` semantic tuples, at the same level as the\n    17\t`:yin.debruijn.code/*` stack image. The register lowerer consumes the\n    18\tresolved tuples and emits a register image; it never reads, rewrites, or\n    19\tdepends on the stack image. The stack image remains independently\n    20\taddressable by its existing H, and the register image is independently\n    21\taddressable by its own R.\n    22\t\n    23\tThis follows the project's established compilation topology\n    24\t(`docs/agents/architecture.md`, \"COMPILATION AS STREAM PROCESSING\"): one\n    25\tupstream stream feeds several parallel projections, and no projection is\n    26\tcompiled from another. That pattern is applied here one level down, at the\n    27\tde Bruijn layer.\n    28\t\n    29\tThis proposal is worth considering on its own merits because it targets an\n    30\talpha-invariant, content-addressed encoding rather than reintroducing a VM\n    31\tdirectly beside the AST walker. It is still at risk of becoming an\n    32\tunnecessary second evaluator. Therefore the lowerer and format may ship\n    33\twithout a kernel, and a kernel is allowed only after the benchmark gate in\n    34\tR3 reports a material benefit over the committed stack VM.\n    35\t\n    36\tThe following invariants apply:\n    37\t\n    38\t1. A register image is derived data, derived from the resolved tuples. The\n    39\t   stack image is never consumed, rewritten, or invalidated by the register\n    40\t   path, and neither image is derived from the other.\n    41\t2. `dao.stream` is the stage boundary. Every lowering is a pure stream-to-\n    42\t   stream function; no stage mutates an input stream or retains a callback.\n    43\t3. H and R are peer format identities, not competing semantic identities.\n    44\t   H identifies the exact stack bytes; R identifies the exact register bytes\n    45\t   that a register-capable host can run. Neither preimage mentions the other.\n    46\t4. Equal R implies equal register bytes and descriptor contract. It does not\n    47\t   imply that a stack VM can execute those bytes.\n    48\t5. Register execution must agree with the named semantic VM, and therefore\n    49\t   with the stack VM, under the B0 normalizer: results, errors, store\n    50\t   effects, stream outcomes, tail calls, and lexical resolution.\n    51\t6. All register allocation choices are data-derived and deterministic across\n    52\t   runs and hosts.\n    53\t\n    54\tThe existing stack design's D1-D16 decisions remain authoritative. The\n    55\tregister path adds no projection fingerprint and no global registry. It\n    56\tproposes one change to the stack design's B2 file box, recorded in section\n    57\t2.2 and deferred to the owner; everything else in `yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`\n    58\tis unchanged.\n    59\t\n    60\t### 1.1 Identity and sharing\n    61\t\n    62\tThe register descriptor is `:yin.debruijn.register/*`. Its `register-hash`\n    63\tfunction, R, hashes the register descriptor hash and the canonical\n    64\tpositional register vector, exactly as B1's `image-hash` hashes the stack\n    65\tdescriptor hash and the canonical stack vector. R is the only identity used\n    66\tto fetch, verify, cache, or execute register bytes. A linker request must\n    67\tname both the format and R, for example\n    68\t`{:format :yin.debruijn.register :hash R}`.\n    69\t\n    70\tR is alpha-invariant for binder renames for the same reason H is: the\n    71\tregister vector carries `:load-bound` depth and position operands and\n    72\t`:closure` arities, and binder names live in a diagnostic side table outside\n    73\tthe hash. Equal R therefore implies agreement on exact scalar spelling, free\n    74\tname operands, and front-end tail flags, as equal H does. Two source\n    75\tprograms that share H share R, and the converse holds, because both are\n    76\tpure functions of the same resolved tuples; this is a testable law, not a\n    77\thashed relation.\n    78\t\n    79\tThis is not a second semantic answer to alpha-equivalence. A register\n    80\tlowerer cannot repair differences in exact scalar spelling, free names, or\n    81\tfront-end tail flags; those differences already make the resolved tuples,\n    82\tand so both H and R, differ.\n    83\t\n    84\tThe pairing of an R with the H of the same named root is a composition\n    85\tfact, carried beside a request or recorded as a datom by whoever publishes\n    86\tboth, never inside either preimage. A receiver verifies a fetched register\n    87\timage with R alone and never needs to have seen the stack image.\n    88\t\n    89\t## 2. Compilation topology and stream contract\n    90\t\n    91\tThe register projection is a peer of the stack projection. Both branch from\n    92\tthe resolved tuples, the de Bruijn encoding of the named AST datoms:\n    93\t\n    94\t    Source -> Yang -> named AST datoms (:yin/*)\n    95\t                         |\\\n    96\t                         | \\-> named semantic image (:yin.code/*)\n    97\t                         |\n    98\t                         +----> resolved tuples (de Bruijn AST, no H)\n    99\t                                   |\\\n   100\t                                   | \\-> stack image (H)      B1 shape, B2\n   101\t                                   |\n   102\t                                   +---> register image (R)   R1\n   103\t\n   104\tThe register edge is not chained after the stack image. The named stream,\n   105\tthe resolved tuples, the stack image, and the register image coexist and\n   106\tremain independently queryable or persistable, per the pipeline invariant\n   107\tthat no stage destroys its input.\n   108\t\n   109\tThe stages have the pure shapes:\n   110\t\n   111\t    (resolve named-datom-stream)      -> resolved-tuple-stream\n   112\t    (lower-stack resolved-stream)     -> stack-image-stream     (H)\n   113\t    (lower-register resolved-stream)  -> register-image-stream  (R)\n   114\t\n   115\tEach register output item carries a register descriptor reference, the\n   116\tcanonical register vector, R, and a diagnostic side table. The implementation\n   117\tmay build indexes while evaluating one bounded item, but those indexes are\n   118\tlocal function data and are not written back into any input.\n   119\t\n   120\tThe register stage refuses malformed resolved tuples, unsupported node\n   121\ttypes, or out-of-range bound operands with qualified data diagnostics. It\n   122\tdoes not silently fall back inside the lowerer. A composition may route the\n   123\tsame named root to the stack path after receiving that outcome.\n   124\t\n   125\t### 2.1 The shared artifact: resolved tuples\n   126\t\n   127\tThe resolved tuples are the named `:yin/*` datoms with exactly two changes,\n   128\tthe same two changes B1's opcode table makes to the named instruction table:\n   129\t\n   130\t1. Every `:variable` node is resolved by the public\n   131\t   `yin.vm.debruijn/resolve-name` against the innermost-first stack of\n   132\t   enclosing parameter vectors, to either a bound reference `[depth\n   133\t   position]` or a free reference carrying the exact symbol.\n   134\t2. Every `:lambda` node carries its arity instead of its parameter vector.\n   135\t\n   136\tEverything else is carried exactly: scalar values with their exact spelling,\n   137\tfree names as supplied, `:yin/tail?` as the front end wrote it, node\n   138\tstructure, and evaluation order. Binder names and provenance go to a\n   139\tdiagnostic side table keyed by node, as B2's side table is keyed by pc.\n   140\tNothing is canonicalized: this is not the dormant merged projection, which\n   141\tcanonicalizes scalars, NFC-folds names, and drops tail flags, and which the\n   142\tstack design's architecture B rejected as a lowering input for those reasons.\n   143\t\n   144\tThe resolver is the tree-level home of name resolution. It validates each\n   145\tbound reference against the enclosing arity chain, the same nonnegative-\n   146\tdepth-and-position rule B2 checks on the vector, and refuses a program whose\n   147\tresolution fails before either lowerer runs. It reuses only\n   148\t`resolve-name`; projection-only helpers are not reused, and the resolver\n   149\tinherits the stack design's retirement condition for that helper.\n   150\t\n   151\tThe resolved tuples have a shape contract and a namespace so that two\n   152\tlowerers and their tests can agree on what they consume. They have no hash,\n   153\tno identity, and no sharing role:\n   154\t\n   155\t- Invariant I shares executable code. The resolved tuples are not executable\n   156\t  by any VM in this project; the executable artifacts are the stack image\n   157\t  and the register image, identified by H and R.\n   158\t- D9 and D12 make H the only identity on the stack sharing path, computed by\n   159\t  one function over the descriptor hash and the canonical vector. R mirrors\n   160\t  that rule for the register path. A hash of the resolved tuples would be a\n   161\t  third identity that no request, response, verification, or cache needs.\n   162\t- \"Derive, do not persist\" (`datom.world.md`): the resolved tuples are a\n   163\t  pure function of the named datoms and `resolve-name`. Persisting them or\n   164\t  giving them an identity would duplicate authority over facts the named\n   165\t  datoms already hold. They are a stage value: computed, consumed, and\n   166\t  discardable. A composition that chooses to retain the stream may do so,\n   167\t  as it may retain any intermediate, but nothing downstream keys on it.\n   168\t- D2 and section 7.3 of the stack design forbid a second lossless record\n   169\t  DAG. The resolved tuples are not one: they are never stored as a source\n   170\t  view, never served, and never a fetch key. With the side table they are\n   171\t  invertible to the named datoms, but that property is used only by tests.\n   172\t\n   173\t### 2.2 Relation to B2 as implemented\n   174\t\n   175\tB2 today produces the stack image in one fused pass: it runs the named\n   176\tlinearizer `yin.vm.linearize/lower`, converts the result to the canonical\n   177\tnamed vector, reconstructs body scopes from that vector, and rewrites `:var`\n   178\tto `:load-bound` or `:load-free` and `:closure` parameters to arity. Its de\n   179\tBruijn addressing therefore happens after stack-shape assembly, over an\n   180\talready push-and-argc shaped vector. There is no point inside B2 at which an\n   181\texecution-model-agnostic de Bruijn form exists.\n   182\t\n   183\tThe peer topology above does not require that file box to change in order\n   184\tto ship R0-R2. B2's fused pass and the two-stage path `lower-stack` after\n   185\t`resolve` are extensionally equal: the named linearizer's walk order,\n   186\toccurrence expansion, and body layout are what `lower-stack` reproduces,\n   187\tand resolution commutes with that walk because it reads only the enclosing\n   188\tparameter stack. The register design therefore binds the two by a tested\n   189\tlaw rather than by construction:\n   190\t\n   191\t    addresses(B2 stack image) = addresses(register image)\n   192\t                              = addresses(resolved tuples)\n   193\t\n   194\twhere `addresses` is the sequence of resolved variable references, bound\n   195\t`[depth position]` or free name, in evaluation order: the main sequence\n   196\tfirst, then each out-of-line body in discovery order. The register lowerer\n   197\tpins that same body layout order so the three sequences align position by\n   198\tposition. This law is an R0 fixture over the whole B0 parity corpus.\n   199\t\n   200\tRecommended, and deferred to the owner because it edits the stack design's\n   201\tsection 3 and B2 box: refactor B2, while it is still uncommitted, into\n   202\t`resolve` followed by a stack linearization of the resolved tuples. That\n   203\tremoves B2's vector-level scope reconstruction (`closure-body-ranges`,\n   204\t`layout-conforms?`, `body-owner`, `chain-of`), which exists only because\n   205\tresolution was placed after linearization, and makes the stack image\n   206\tliterally a peer projection rather than an equivalent one. The cost is a\n   207\tcopy of the named linearizer's flattening walk in the stack lowerer, since\n   208\t`lower` cannot consume resolved tuples, and the loss of by-construction\n   209\tlayout equality with `lower`, which becomes the existing structural-\n   210\tcomparison test instead. Every golden H is unchanged either way, because the\n   211\temitted bytes are identical; the address law and B1's golden fixtures prove\n   212\tit. If the owner declines, B2 stays as is and the address law is the only\n   213\tcoupling.\n   214\t\n   215\t## 3. Register dimension\n   216\t\n   217\tThe descriptor declares:\n   218\t\n   219\t    +----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   220\t    | Field                | Rule                                          |\n   221\t    +----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   222\t    | Namespace            | :yin.debruijn.register/*                      |\n   223\t    | Contract version     | Explicit integer, included in descriptor H    |\n   224\t    | Source               | Resolved tuples (section 2.1)                 |\n   225\t    | Register image       | Body ranges plus positional instructions      |\n   226\t    | Scalar encoding      | Reuse B1 scalar bytes, never projection NFC   |\n   227\t    | Lexical addressing   | :load-bound depth/position remains explicit   |\n   228\t    | Free addressing      | :load-free name remains exact                 |\n   229\t    | Validation           | Shape, targets, body scope, register bounds   |\n   230\t    | Lift                 | Register image to named semantics             |\n   231\t    +----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   232\t\n   233\tThe register descriptor has its own validator because register operands and\n   234\tbody register counts are new facts. It reuses B1's public data rules and\n   235\tscalar encoder only through an explicit dependency; it does not call private\n   236\tprojection helpers. The descriptor version is incremented whenever opcode\n   237\tshape, allocation, scalar framing, or control-flow rules change.\n   238\t\n   239\tThe lowerer's R formula is:\n   240\t\n   241\t    R = sha256(register-descriptor-hash || canonical-register-vector)\n   242\t\n   243\tBoth components are canonical bytes. Received register bytes are hashed\n   244\tbefore decoding, as in B1's wire rule. A receiver verifies R, checks the\n   245\tdescriptor, runs the receiver closure check over `:load-free` operands\n   246\texactly as D11 and D15 define it for stack images, validates, and only then\n   247\texecutes. No relation to a stack H is embedded or checked.\n   248\t\n   249\tThe lift goes from the register image to the named `:yin.code/*` image with\n   250\tdiagnostic or synthesized binder names, so that B6's rule \"a fetched image\n   251\tmay be executed by the semantic VM after lifting\" holds for register images\n   252\ttoo. It is a function of the register image plus its side table.\n   253\t\n   254\t## 4. Resolved-tuples-to-register lowering\n   255\t\n   256\t### 4.1 Input and validation\n   257\t\n   258\tThe lowerer accepts only resolved tuples from the section 2.1 resolver and\n   259\ttheir side table. It does not accept named datoms directly, does not rerun\n   260\tname resolution, and does not accept a stack image. B2 remains the only\n   261\tproducer of `:yin.debruijn.code/*` stack images; this lowerer is the only\n   262\tproducer of `:yin.debruijn.register/*` register images.\n   263\t\n   264\tBecause the input is a tree, there is no control-flow graph to recover and\n   265\tno abstract operand stack to simulate: every intermediate value is an\n   266\texpression whose extent is known from the tree. The walk is the named\n   267\tlinearizer's order, operator then operands left to right, lambda bodies out\n   268\tof line in discovery order, `if` evaluating one arm. Every body has one\n   269\tenclosing lexical chain by construction, and the resolver has already\n   270\tvalidated every bound reference against it. The lowerer's own checks are\n   271\tregister bounds, body ranges, and target bounds, applied to its output.\n   272\t\n   273\t### 4.2 Register classes\n   274\t\n   275\tEach body has two disjoint register banks:\n   276\t\n   277\t    L0 ... L(n-1)     current body's parameters\n   278\t    T0 ... T(k-1)     expression temporaries\n   279\t\n   280\t`n` is the body's declared arity. `:load-bound rd depth position` reads the\n   281\texisting lexical frame chain and writes `rd`; it does not assign an outer\n   282\tframe's slot a register number in the current body. Thus frame 0 remains the\n   283\tinnermost frame and the runtime frame vector remains outermost-first, exactly\n   284\tas in B3. `:load-free rd name` preserves the existing resolution order.\n   285\t\n   286\tThe allocator gives every intermediate expression value a virtual temporary\n   287\tin evaluation order, then performs deterministic linear-scan allocation\n   288\twithin each body. Locals are never coalesced with temporaries. The physical\n   289\tregister count is the number of local registers plus the maximum\n   290\tsimultaneously live temporary registers. If a target requires a bounded\n   291\tregister file, deterministic spill slots are allocated after the last\n   292\tregister using the same order; spilling is a later kernel concern and is not\n   293\timplicit in R1.\n   294\t\n   295\t### 4.3 Determinism\n   296\t\n   297\tLowering from a tree removes the join-order risk a stack-image compiler\n   298\twould have: there are no predecessor traversals to order, and both arms of\n   299\tan `if` write the expression's one destination register. The remaining\n   300\trisks are hash-map iteration, liveness tie breaks, and spill choices.\n   301\t\n   302\tThe lowerer closes these risks by using evaluation order for virtual-value\n   303\tdefinition, lowest available register, and lowest virtual-id tie breaks.\n   304\tAny moves that a target convention requires are sorted by destination\n   305\tregister and emitted in a fixed cycle-breaking order. No host map iteration\n   306\torder may affect an emitted vector. R1 pins golden register vectors and R\n   307\tvalues for a corpus on JVM, CLJS, and CLJD.\n   308\t\n   309\t### 4.4 Instruction mapping\n   310\t\n   311\tThe register instruction set is a positional form of the B1 table:\n   312\t\n   313\t    :const        [op rd value]\n   314\t    :load-bound   [op rd depth position]\n   315\t    :load-free    [op rd name]\n   316\t    :closure      [op rd arity body-pc]\n   317\t    :move         [op rd rs]\n   318\t    :call         [op rd fn-reg arg-regs tail?]\n   319\t    :branch-false [op cond-reg target]\n   320\t    :jump         [op target]\n   321\t    :return       [op value-reg]\n   322\t    :halt         [op value-reg]\n   323\t    :store-get    [op rd key]\n   324\t    :store-put    [op key value-reg]\n   325\t\n   326\tThere is no `:push`: operands are named by register, so the stack path's\n   327\tpush-before-each-operand convention never arises. `:call` differs most from\n   328\tits stack form: instead of an argc over an operand stack it names an\n   329\texplicit function register, an ordered argument-register vector, a\n   330\tdestination register, and the tail flag copied from `:yin/tail?`.\n   331\t`:branch-false`, `:return`, and `:halt` name registers instead of reading an\n   332\toperand stack. Constants, lexical loads, free loads, closures, jumps, and\n   333\tstore operations retain their semantic operands while gaining explicit\n   334\tdestinations where needed.\n   335\t\n   336\tStream, gensym, FFI, park, and resume instructions use the same explicit\n   337\tdestination and source-register convention in R2. Their effect descriptors\n   338\tremain stream values; no callback or hidden scheduler is introduced.\n   339\t\n   340\t## 5. Execution boundary\n   341\t\n   342\tR1 may produce and validate register bytecode without a register kernel. This\n   343\tis the recommended first milestone and is analogous to B1's standalone\n   344\tdimension, but the register format is not a promise that a second evaluator\n   345\twill exist.\n   346\t\n   347\tIf the benchmark gate passes, R3 may add\n   348\t`yin.vm.debruijn.register` as a sibling kernel. It owns explicit state\n   349\t`{:image :pc :registers :frames :free-env :continuation :store :status\n   350\t:primitives :modules}` and uses the same frame, free-name, store, stream, and\n   351\tcontinuation contracts as B3, including the engine seam B4 implements\n   352\t(`yin.vm.engine.md`: one restore function, per-site park-entry builders, the\n   353\tengine-owned key sets). It adds no `IVM` or `IVMState` methods.\n   354\t\n   355\tThe register kernel must execute only validator-approved images. It must\n   356\tpreserve B3's frame direction, closure capture, nil-fill and extra-argument\n   357\trules, and B0 normalization. Register continuations are not interchangeable\n   358\twith stack continuations unless an explicit lift is provided.\n   359\t\n   360\t## 6. Implementation phases\n   361\t\n   362\tEach phase has a bounded file box, must-not-change list, completion criteria,\n   363\tand JVM, CLJS, CLJD, kondo, and cljstyle verification.\n   364\t\n   365\t### R0: contract and corpus\n   366\t\n   367\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_contract_test.cljc\n   368\t    Existing edits: none\n   369\t    Must not change: B0-B3, named datoms, stack image, resolver contract,\n   370\t    projection namespace\n   371\t\n   372\tThe resolver (`yin.vm.debruijn-resolve`: resolved tuples, side table,\n   373\tscope validation, the inverse used by tests) is now B2's own file box, per\n   374\tthe stack design's B2 phase box (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` section 3.1) --\n   375\tnot built here. R0 depends on it and freezes the register descriptor\n   376\tversion, operand mapping, normalizer fixtures, and the named-root corpus\n   377\tagainst it. Completion requires every node type from the resolver either\n   378\tmapped or refused with a named diagnostic, the duplicate-parameter and\n   379\tfree-name fixtures from B2, the section 2.2 address law between B2's stack\n   380\timages and the resolved tuples over the B0 parity corpus, pure data\n   381\tchecks, and a decision that no register kernel is assumed yet.\n   382\t\n   383\t### R1: register dimension and lowerer\n   384\t\n   385\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc\n   386\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile.cljc\n   387\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile_test.cljc\n   388\t    Existing edits: none\n   389\t    Must not change: B1 code, B2 lowerer, B3 VM, resolver contract,\n   390\t    dao.stream protocols\n   391\t\n   392\tImplement the descriptor, validation, deterministic tree lowering,\n   393\tallocation, encoding, R, and the lift to `:yin.code/*`. Completion requires\n   394\tevery resolved node type either mapped or refused with a named diagnostic,\n   395\tbyte-identical repeated lowerings, golden register vectors, cross-host R\n   396\tagreement, the section 2.2 address law extended to the register image, and\n   397\tthe lift law `lift(lower-register (resolve x), side-table)` alpha-equivalent\n   398\tto `canonical-vector(lower x)` under the arity and layout caveats of the\n   399\tstack design's B2 box.\n   400\t\n   401\t### R2: effects and stream forms\n   402\t\n   403\t    Existing source: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile.cljc\n   404\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects_test.cljc\n   405\t    Existing edits: none\n   406\t    Must not change: dao.stream, lease, waitset, B0-B3 semantics\n   407\t\n   408\tLower stream, gensym, FFI, park, and resume shapes with explicit registers.\n   409\tCompletion compares effect descriptors and blocked outcomes with the stack\n   410\tpath without executing a register VM.\n   411\t\n   412\t### R3: benchmark gate\n   413\t\n   414\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_benchmark_test.cljc\n   415\t    Existing edits: none\n   416\t    Must not change: stack VM and its H\n   417\t\n   418\tRun identical pure-program corpora through the stack VM and a reference\n   419\tregister interpreter or instrumentation harness. Report throughput,\n   420\tallocation, image size, load time, and lowering cost. A kernel is not\n   421\tauthorized merely because R1 passes; the report must show a material benefit\n   422\tor the register path remains a lowerer-only artifact.\n   423\t\n   424\t### R4: optional register kernel\n   425\t\n   426\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc\n   427\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn/register_test.cljc\n   428\t    Existing edits: none\n   429\t    Must not change: B0-B3, named VM, existing IVM methods\n   430\t\n   431\tImplement only if R3 justifies it. Completion requires B0-normalized parity\n   432\tagainst the named VM and the stack VM, all register validator fixtures,\n   433\tlexical and closure tests, store and control flow tests, and deterministic\n   434\tstream/effect behavior.\n   435\t\n   436\t### R5: linker integration\n   437\t\n   438\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_linker.cljc\n   439\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_linker_test.cljc\n   440\t    Existing edits: none\n   441\t    Must not change: stack linker semantics or stack H\n   442\t\n   443\tFetch and verify R over `dao.stream` with the same request, wire-hash,\n   444\tclosure-check, and refusal shape as B6, keyed by R. A host may refuse R and\n   445\tinstead request, by H, a stack image the composition has published for the\n   446\tsame named root; that pairing is composition data, not linker machinery. No\n   447\tglobal loader or callback is introduced.\n   448\t\n   449\t## 7. Non-goals and protected surfaces\n   450\t\n   451\tThis design does not alter any B0-B7 phase's semantics, the stack VM, named\n   452\tdatoms, the dormant projection, or the AST-walker. It does not change B1's\n   453\tH, its scalar bytes, its validator, or its wire protocol. It does not add\n   454\tequality saturation, a JIT, lambda lifting, ANF, global distribution, or a\n   455\tnew continuation interchange format. Its one proposed edit to the stack\n   456\tdesign, B2's adoption of the resolver, is recorded in section 2.2 and is the\n   457\towner's decision.\n   458\t\n   459\tThe register lowerer consumes the resolved tuples, never the stack image and\n   460\tnever named datoms directly. It is not a second stack lowerer: it emits only\n   220\tLowering is two stages, both pure stream-to-stream functions:\n   221\t\n   222\t    (resolve named-datoms)          -> resolved tuples + binder side table\n   223\t    (lower-stack resolved-tuples)   -> canonical stack image + pc side table\n   224\t\n   225\t`resolve` is the de Bruijn encoding of the named semantic tuples. It is the\n   226\tshared upstream of the stack projection specified here and the register\n   227\tprojection specified in `yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`; neither projection\n   228\tis derived from the other, matching the parallel-projection topology in\n   229\t`docs/agents/architecture.md`. `resolve` receives complete `:yin/*` named\n   230\tdatoms and reuses only the public `yin.vm.debruijn/resolve-name` helper.\n   231\tThat helper compares names exactly, including rightmost-wins duplicate\n   232\tparameters, and does not canonicalize values. Projection-only helpers such\n   233\tas `index-frame`, `build-node`, and `project-node` are not reused.\n   234\tUnexpanded-macro validation remains the named front end's responsibility.\n   235\tThe projection is therefore kept, not removed: if it is ever retired, this\n   236\tpublic helper must first move into the resolver's namespace.\n   237\t\n   238\t### 3.1 Resolved tuples\n   239\t\n   240\tThe resolved tuples are the named datoms with exactly two changes, the same\n   241\ttwo changes the B1 opcode table makes to the named instruction table. Every\n   242\tother datom is carried unchanged: entity ids, `:yin/type`, operands, exact\n   243\tscalar values, exact free names, `:yin/tail?`, and node structure. Entity\n   244\tids are preserved, so a resolved node's provenance is its own id and no\n   245\tseparate provenance table exists at this level.\n   246\t\n   247\t1. A `:variable` node loses its `:yin/name` datom and gains either\n   248\t   `:yin.resolved/depth` and `:yin.resolved/position` (a bound reference,\n   249\t   from `resolve-name`'s `{:bound [depth position]}`) or `:yin.resolved/free`\n   250\t   carrying the exact symbol (from `{:free name}`).\n   251\t2. A `:lambda` node loses its `:yin/params` datom and gains\n   252\t   `:yin.resolved/arity`, the parameter count.\n   253\t\n   254\tThe attribute namespace is `:yin.resolved/*`, not `:yin.debruijn/*`, which\n   255\tthe dormant projection owns. The binder side table is `{lambda-entity\n   256\t[param-symbol ...]}`: the exact parameter vector of every lambda, keyed by\n   257\tits entity. No name is recorded per variable occurrence; a bound occurrence's\n   258\toriginal spelling is the owning lambda's parameter at the resolved position,\n   259\twhich `resolve-name`'s contract guarantees is exact.\n   260\t\n   261\tThe walk is a tree walk in the named linearizer's order: operator then\n   262\toperands left to right, `if` test then arms, lambda bodies with the lambda's\n   263\tparameter vector pushed innermost-first on the resolution stack for the\n   264\tbody's extent. Depth 0 is the innermost enclosing lambda. This stack is\n   265\tthe resolver's only state; the runtime frame vector's outermost-first order\n   266\tis B3's, and the conversion between them is explicit there.\n   267\t\n   268\t`resolve` refuses with a qualified diagnostic, before any lowerer runs, a\n   269\tprogram whose bound reference falls outside its enclosing arity chain, an\n   270\tunsupported node type, or a host value in a data operand. A hand-built\n   271\tresolved tuple set with `:yin.resolved/depth 5` and no enclosing lambda is\n   272\trejected by `resolve`'s own validator, which walks the tree once more\n   273\tagainst the arity chain.\n   274\t\n   275\tThe resolved tuples are a derived stage value. They have this shape\n   276\tcontract and namespace so that two lowerers and their tests agree on what\n   277\tthey consume, but no hash, no identity, and no sharing role: H and R are\n   278\tthe only executable identities (D9, D12), the tuples are not executable,\n   279\tand \"derive, do not persist\" forbids a persisted copy of facts the named\n   280\tdatoms already hold. They are not the second lossless view D2 rejects:\n   281\tnever stored as a source view, never served, never a fetch key. With the\n   282\tbinder side table they invert to the named datoms; that inverse is the\n   283\tresolver's test oracle only.\n   284\t\n   285\t### 3.2 Stack lowering\n   286\t\n   287\t`lower-stack` consumes resolved tuples and emits the canonical positional\n   288\t`:yin.debruijn.code/*` image (section 2) plus the pc-keyed diagnostic side\n   289\ttable the lift needs. It cannot call `yin.vm.linearize/lower`, which reads\n   290\t`:yin/name` and `:yin/params`. It therefore reproduces the named\n   291\tlinearizer's flattening itself: the same recursion in evaluation order, the\n   292\tsame `:push` after operator and after each operand, `:call` with argc and\n   293\tthe copied `:yin/tail?`, `if` as `branch-false`, `jump`, and two labels,\n   294\t`:halt` after the root, lambda bodies out of line in discovery order each\n   295\tending in `:return`, and labels resolved to pcs in a second pass. A\n   296\t`:variable` emits `[:load-bound depth position]` or `[:load-free name]`\n   297\tstraight from its resolved attributes; a `:lambda` emits `[:closure arity\n   298\tbody-pc]`. Every other node emits the carried mnemonic unchanged.\n   299\t\n   300\tLayout equality with the named linearizer no longer holds by construction.\n   301\tIt is a required structural-comparison test: for every corpus program the\n   302\tcanonical vector of `(lower x)` and `(lower-stack (resolve x))` have the\n   303\tsame length and the same mnemonic at every pc, and differ only at `:var`\n   304\tversus `:load-bound`/`:load-free` operands and at `:closure`'s parameter\n   305\tversus arity operand. A layout change in `yin.vm.linearize` must fail this\n   306\ttest rather than silently fork H.\n   307\t\n   308\t`lower-stack` copies `:yin/tail?` exactly as it was resolved. It does not\n   309\tinfer tail position from syntax. Only `:application` has a tail operand in\n   310\tthe existing instruction table; `:dao.stream.apply/call` lowers to\n   311\t`:ffi-call` and has no tail operand.\n   312\t\n   313\tThe pc side table is `{pc {:kind :closure, :params [sym ...], :source e}}`\n   314\tfor every `:closure` pc, read from the binder side table by lambda entity,\n   315\tand `{pc {:kind :var, :source e}}` for every load pc. `adapt` is the\n   316\tcomposition `lower-stack` after `resolve` and is the one public entry that\n   317\ttakes named datoms to an image.\n   318\t\n   319\tThis removes the fused design's vector-level scope reconstruction:\n   320\t`closure-body-ranges`, `layout-conforms?`, `body-owner`, `chain-of`, and\n   321\tthe vector-level `resolve-var` and `rewrite`. That machinery existed only\n   322\tbecause resolution was placed after linearization and had to recover body\n   323\tscopes from an already flattened vector. Scope is now known during the tree\n   324\twalk, and B1's validator remains the independent vector-level check.\n   325\t\n   326\tThe executable image may carry a debug node hash and named source reference.\n   327\tThose fields are always diagnostic metadata outside code identity. They replace\n   328\tno named source map.\n   329\t\n   330\tScope validation is mandatory in both places where an image can enter:\n   331\t\n   332\t1. `resolve` validates nonnegative depth and position against the enclosing\n   333\t   arity chain at resolution time, on the tree.\n   334\t2. B1's image validator repeats the check by walking each body's declared\n   335\t   arity and enclosing-body chain. A shape-valid hand-built image with\n   336\t   `[:load-bound [5 0]]` is rejected before execution.\n   337\t\n   338\tNo projected reader participates in this path; only the B1 image validator\n   339\tadmits executable images.\n   340\t\n   490\trows,\n   491\tout-of-range bound operands, exact spelling preservation, distinct hashes\n   492\tfor composed and decomposed e-acute, a ratio fixture that is either\n   493\tencoded or refused with `:unsupported-value` (JVM encodes, CLJS and\n   494\tClojureDart both refuse), distinct hashes for `1`/`1.0` on JVM and Dart,\n   495\tdistinct hashes for ratios and chars on the JVM, and identical bytes\n   496\tacross hosts for the common scalar domain. Golden image bytes and H\n   497\tvalues for a frozen corpus are checked on all three host lanes. A\n   498\tlowering layout change must fail those fixtures rather than silently\n   499\tforking identity.\n   500\t\n   501\t### B2: resolver and stack lowerer\n   502\t\n   503\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_resolve.cljc\n   504\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_resolve_test.cljc\n   505\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc\n   506\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize_test.cljc\n   507\t    Existing edits: none\n   508\t    Must not change: yin.vm.linearize and named lowering semantics\n   509\t\n   510\tB2 is two namespaces (section 3). `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve` owns `resolve`:\n   511\tnamed datoms to resolved tuples plus binder side table, its scope validator,\n   512\tand the inverse `unresolve` used as a test oracle. It is shared with the\n   513\tregister design and depends on nothing in this design below section 3.1.\n   514\t`yin.vm.debruijn-linearize` owns `lower-stack`, `adapt`, `lift`, and the\n   515\tpublic `named-canonical-vector` helper the structural comparison uses. The\n   516\tfused implementation's `closure-body-ranges`, `layout-conforms?`,\n   517\t`body-owner`, `chain-of`, vector-level `resolve-var`, `rewrite`, and the\n   518\tduplicate vector-level `image-scope-defect` are removed, not moved: scope\n   519\tis known on the tree, and B1's validator is the vector-level check.\n   520\t\n   521\tCompletion requires, for the resolver: deterministic output on every host,\n   522\tevery node type resolved or refused with a named diagnostic, exact free\n   523\tnames and scalars, the duplicate-parameter fixture `(fn [x x] x)` resolving\n   524\tto `[0 1]`, out-of-range hand-built resolved tuples refused, and\n   525\t`unresolve(resolve x, side-table) = x` over the corpus.\n   526\t\n   527\tFor the stack lowerer: deterministic output, every node and opcode, the\n   528\tsection 3.2 structural opcode-by-opcode comparison with `lower` differing\n   529\tonly at variable and closure operands, image encode/validate/load round\n   530\ttrips, and `lift(adapt x, side-table) = canonical-vector(lower x)`. The lift\n   531\tis a function of the image plus its pc side table. With the original binder\n   532\tnames in the side table, equality holds; with synthesized names, the lifted\n   533\tresult is alpha-equivalent to `lower x`, including `(fn [x x] x)`.\n   534\tSynthesized names must be fresh against the image's free-name set. A\n   535\tsupplied side table is accepted only when `adapt` of its lift returns the\n   536\toriginal image. Every image B2 emits must be accepted by B1's validator, and\n   537\tevery hand-built out-of-range image must be rejected by B1's validator. The\n   538\tgolden image bytes and H values frozen in B1 must be reproduced unchanged\n   539\tby `adapt`; the refactor changes derivation, not bytes.\n   540\t\n   541\t### B3: de Bruijn VM kernel\n   542\t\n   543\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc\n   544\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_test.cljc\n   545\t    Existing edits: none\n   546\t    Must not change: semantic VM, engine, IVM protocols, named environment,\n   547\t    merged projection namespace\n   548\t\n   549\tImplement frames, closures, loads, calls, returns, branches, literals, and\n   550\tstore operations. Completion requires pure-program parity using B0's\n   551\tnormalizer and fresh initial environments, plus the informational section 1\n   552\tbenchmark report, not an acceptance condition.\n   553\t\n   554\t### B4: effects and continuations\n   555\t\n   556\t    Existing source: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc\n   557\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_effects_test.cljc\n   558\t    Existing edits: src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc (additive only:\n   559\t      scheduler-round; resume-from-run-queue calls restore-fn with three\n   560\t      arguments), src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc (additive only:\n   561\t      response-wait-entry), test/yin/vm/engine_test.cljc (fake restore\n   562\t      arity; tests for the two additions)\n   563\t    Must not change: dao.stream protocols, lease, waitset, named effect rules,\n   564\t    merged projection namespace, yin.vm.semantic, yin.vm.ast_walker (B4's\n   565\t    parity oracles; their migration to the engine additions is a separate\n   566\t    commit), any existing engine outcome or entry key\n   567\t\n   568\tImplement stream operations, primitives, FFI, gensym, current-continuation,\n   569\tpark, and resume against the engine seam in section 4.1 and\n   570\t`yin.vm.engine.md`. The engine edits are exactly the three listed in that\n   571\tdocument's section 7; B4 adds no other engine function and hand-writes no\n   572\tscheduler round, run-queue wrapper, or two-arity restore shim. Completion\n   573\trequires parity for values, errors, effects, stores, stream outcomes, and\n   574\tblocked states. Cross-program park/resume tests must use only\n   575\tinitial-environment or store-resolved free names until the named-VM\n   576\tenvironment leak is fixed. Before a continuation is exported, B4 must either\n   577\tlift frames through the descriptor morphism or run the frame-aware\n   578\tcompletion adapter so `:yin.k/requires` is not under-approximated.\n   579\t\n   580\t### B5: differential integration\n   581\t\n   582\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_parity_test.cljc\n   583\t    Existing edits: none\n   584\t    Must not change: named storage and the existing linearizer pipeline\n   585\t\n     1\t# yin.vm engine: the VM seam\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: contract of the existing `yin.vm.engine` namespace as of 2026-09-22,\n     4\tplus three additive engine edits proposed for B4 of\n     5\t`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`. No protocol is introduced; see section 6.\n     6\t\n     7\t`yin.vm.engine` is the shared scheduler for every Yin VM: the dispatch loop,\n     8\twait-set polling, ready-queue resumption, park and resume records, and the\n     9\tstream-effect handlers. It is VM-agnostic because every place it needs to\n    10\tknow a VM's register layout it takes a function argument. This document\n    11\tnames that seam precisely so the third VM (B4) and any later one (R4) are\n    12\twritten against a stated contract rather than by copying the last VM.\n    13\t\n    14\t## 1. What the engine owns\n    15\t\n    16\tFunctions, all already VM-shape-agnostic:\n    17\t\n    18\t    run-loop                state active? step-fn resume-fn -> state\n    19\t    check-wait-set          state -> state          (poll, wake onto ready-queue)\n    20\t    resume-from-run-queue   state restore-fn -> state | nil\n    21\t    park-continuation       state cont-fields -> state\n    22\t    resume-continuation     state parked-id val restore-fn -> state\n    23\t    handle-effect           state effect {:park-entry-fns ...} -> outcome map\n    24\t    gensym, handle-make/put/cursor/next/close, handle-stream-block\n    25\t\n    26\tBookkeeping keys the engine reads or writes on the VM state map. A VM that\n    27\tmaintains these gets `run-loop`, `active-continuation?`,\n    28\t`halted-with-empty-queue?`, `vm-blocked?`, and `vm-value` for free:\n    29\t\n    30\t    :blocked?  :halted?  :wait-set  :ready-queue  :parked\n    31\t    :id-counter  :store  :value  :make-stream  :modules\n    32\t\n    33\tKnown vocabulary leak: `ready-for-ingress?` also reads `:k`, `:control`, and\n    34\t`:bytecode`, the named VMs' register names. It is the observer's idle gate,\n    35\tnot scheduler machinery, and a VM without those keys that is ever placed\n    36\tunder `dao.stream.observer` must supply its own idle predicate. B4 does not\n    37\tconsume it.\n    38\t\n    39\tThe program-cache helpers (`pinned-compiled-versions` and friends) walk\n    40\t`:parked`, `:ready-queue`, and `:wait-set` looking for `:compiled-version`\n    41\tinside `:next`-chained frames. On a payload without those keys they find\n    42\tnothing, which is correct; no VM in this repository uses the compiled cache.\n    43\t\n    44\t## 2. What a VM supplies\n    45\t\n    46\tExactly one VM-level function and one instruction-site builder shape.\n    47\t\n    48\t### 2.1 The restore function\n    49\t\n    50\t    restore : base entry val -> state\n    51\t\n    52\t`base` is the VM state after the engine has done its part: the ready entry\n    53\tpopped, `:store-updates` merged, `:blocked?` and `:halted?` cleared (from\n    54\t`resume-from-run-queue`), or the parked record removed (from\n    55\t`resume-continuation`). `entry` is the wait, ready, or parked record. `val`\n    56\tis the value the parked instruction receives: the woken stream value, or the\n    57\tresume operand.\n    58\t\n    59\tThe restore writes the VM's registers from the entry's register keys and\n    60\tplaces `val` where the instruction after the park point expects its result.\n    61\tIt leaves the bookkeeping keys as `base` has them. The one exception is the\n    62\tFFI two-step in section 4, which may re-park.\n    63\t\n    64\tEvery restore ever written in this repository has this signature: the\n    65\twalker's `ast-walker-restore`, the semantic VM's `semantic-restore`, and\n    66\tv1's `semantic-vm-restore`, `stack-vm-restore`, and `register-vm-restore`\n    67\t(deleted in `d8b27a50`). The only variation is a two-arity shim\n    68\t`([base entry] (restore base entry (:value entry)))` that exists because\n    69\t`resume-from-run-queue` calls the function with two arguments and\n    70\t`resume-continuation` with three. That is a defect of the engine's call\n    71\tconvention, not a VM choice, and section 7 removes it.\n    72\t\n    73\t### 2.2 The park-entry builder\n    74\t\n    75\t    park-entry : state effect result -> entry\n    76\t\n    77\tHanded to `handle-effect` under `:park-entry-fns` keyed by `:stream/put` and\n    78\t`:stream/next`. It returns the VM's register payload for the continuation\n    79\tafter the blocking instruction, merged with the transport keys the engine\n    80\treads:\n    81\t\n    82\t    (merge registers {:reason :put   :stream-id id})\n    83\t    (merge registers {:reason :next  :stream-id id :cursor-ref ref})\n    84\t\n    85\t`handle-effect` stamps `:datom` onto a `:put` entry if the builder did not.\n    86\t\n    87\tBuilders close over instruction-site locals: the semantic VM's over\n    88\t`seg pc E St K` in its hot loop, the walker's over `k env`. They are\n    89\tinstruction-level values, not VM-level ones, which is why no protocol on the\n    90\tVM type can express them and why they stay as closures at the call site.\n    91\t\n    92\t## 3. The entry key contract\n    93\t\n    94\tThis is the actual interface between the engine and a VM. A wait, ready, or\n    95\tparked entry is the union of two disjoint key sets.\n    96\t\n    97\tEngine-owned keys, read or written by the engine and `dao.stream.waitset`:\n    98\t\n    99\t    :reason  :stream-id  :cursor-ref  :datom\n   100\t    :value  :status  :store-updates  :cursor\n   101\t    :type  :id                           (parked records only)\n   102\t\n   103\t`:stream` may appear on a woken entry transiently and is dropped before the\n   104\tentry reaches the ready queue.\n   105\t\n   106\tFFI keys, owned by the `yin.vm.ffi` convention: `:call-id`, `:request-sent`,\n   107\t`:op`.\n   108\t\n   109\tEvery other key is the VM's register payload. The engine never reads,\n   110\twrites, or removes it, and it survives park, poll, wake, and resume\n   111\tverbatim. A VM's register keys must not collide with the sets above.\n   112\t\n   113\tPayloads in use or planned:\n   114\t\n   115\t    +-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   116\t    | VM                    | Register payload                              |\n   117\t    +-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   118\t    | yin.vm.ast_walker     | {:k :env}                                     |\n   119\t    | yin.vm.semantic       | {:segment :pc :env :stack :k}                 |\n   120\t    | yin.vm.debruijn.stack | {:segment :pc :frames :stack :continuation}   |\n   121\t    |   (B4, planned)       |                                               |\n   122\t    | register kernel (R4,  | its own; same rules                           |\n   123\t    |   gated, hypothetical)|                                               |\n   124\t    +-----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   125\t\n   126\tTwo value tags are also shared, because the B0 normalizer compares\n   127\tcontinuations and parked records by type only: a reified continuation is\n   128\t`{:type :reified-continuation ...registers}` and a parked record is\n   129\t`{:type :parked-continuation :id ...registers}` (the engine adds the tag and\n   130\tid in `park-continuation`).\n   131\t\n   132\t## 4. The FFI two-step\n   133\t\n   134\tAn `:ffi-call` parks, appends a request, and then waits twice: first, if the\n   135\tcall-in stream was `full`, as a writer retrying the request; then as a reader\n   136\ton the call-out cursor for the correlated response. Two entry shapes result:\n   137\t\n   138\t- A woken `:request-sent` writer must not resume. Its registers are re-parked\n   139\t  as a call-out reader with `:call-id`, and the machine stays blocked.\n   140\t- A woken `:call-id` reader unwraps its value with `ffi/call-result`, drops\n   141\t  its record from `:parked`, and resumes with the unwrapped value.\n   142\t\n   143\tThe two live VMs place this logic differently. The semantic VM handles both\n   144\tcases inside `semantic-restore`, keyed on top-level entry keys. The walker\n   145\thandles them in its continuation dispatch, keyed on frame types\n   146\t`:dao.stream.apply/request-sent` and `:dao.stream.apply/eval-call` that\n   147\t`ffi/call-response-wait-entry` builds into `:k`. Both are correct; they are\n   148\ttwo placements of the same fifteen lines.\n   149\t\n   150\tB4 uses the semantic VM's placement. The stack VM's continuation is a vector\n   151\tof return frames, not typed CESK frames, so the walker's placement does not\n   152\tfit it. Under the section 3 rule the re-park is register-agnostic: the\n   153\tresponse reader is the writer entry with `:request-sent`, `:op`, and `:datom`\n   154\tremoved and the call-out reader keys added. Section 7 adds that helper.\n   155\t\n   156\tThe two-step is not lifted into the engine now. Doing so would require the\n   157\twalker to abandon its frame-typed placement, which is a refactor of a working\n   158\tVM that B4 does not need. It is the one piece of genuinely duplicated logic\n   159\tat this seam, and it becomes a three-instance extraction candidate once B4\n   160\texists. The section 7 helper makes that extraction mechanical.\n   161\t\n   162\t## 5. Glue that is pure duplication\n   163\t\n   164\tEach VM today writes, in addition to its restore and its builders:\n   165\t\n   166\t    +----------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n   167\t    | Per-VM glue                | Why it exists                             |\n   168\t    +----------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n   169\t    | private resume-from-run-   | engine has no form with restore-fn bound  |\n   170\t    |   queue wrapper (1 line)   |                                           |\n   171\t    | scheduler-round: check-    | engine has no composed form; the walker   |\n   172\t    |   wait-set then resume     | inlines it, the semantic VM names it      |\n   173\t    | two-arity restore shim     | engine calls restore with 2 and 3 args    |\n   174\t    | X-run-scheduler: run-loop  | fine; stays (it binds step-fn, which is   |\n   175\t    |   with step-fn bound       | genuinely per-VM)                         |\n   176\t    +----------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\n   177\t\n   178\tThe first three are eliminated by the section 7 edits. What remains per VM\n   179\tis exactly what must be per VM: the restore, the builders, and a one-line\n   180\trunner binding the VM's step function.\n   181\t\n   182\t## 6. Why not a protocol\n   183\t\n   184\tThe question was whether to formalize the restore-fn and park-entry contract\n   185\tas a protocol, a multimethod, or a required key on the VM state map.\n   186\t\n   187\tThe answer is no, and not because there is only one instance to generalize\n   188\tfrom. That premise is false: two VMs use this seam in production today\n   189\t(`yin.vm.ast_walker` and `yin.vm.semantic`), three did in v1 (semantic,\n   190\tstack, register, all against a v1 engine of the same shape), and in none of\n   191\tthe five did the restore signature vary. The 3-VM case this proposal worries\n   192\tabout was already run, in this codebase, on the bare-function convention,\n   193\twithout a protocol, and the convention held. That is a demonstrated outcome,\n   194\tnot a prediction.\n   195\t\n   196\tWhat the evidence shows is that the VM-specific surface is one function of\n   197\tfixed signature plus builder closures that no VM-level dispatch can reach. A\n   198\tprotocol would replace one positional argument with type dispatch, add an\n   199\t`extend-type` obligation to every VM, and break the engine tests' plain-map\n   200\tstates and fake restore functions, in exchange for nothing the argument does\n   201\tnot already do. A `:yin.vm.engine/restore` key holding the function on the\n   202\tstate map is worse: it puts a closure in VM state, which the v2 port\n   203\tspecifically removed (\"a ready entry holds registers, ids, and values, never\n   204\ta closure\") so that a blocked machine survives an EDN round-trip.\n   205\t\n   206\tThe v1 to v2 history points the same way. v1 threaded `restore-fn` into\n   207\t`handle-effect`, `check-wait-set`, and `run-loop`, and wrapped entries as\n   208\truntime tasks. v2 deleted all of that and left restore dispatch in exactly\n   209\ttwo engine functions. The engine has been getting less abstract at this\n   210\tseam, on purpose. `datom.world.md`: interpretation over abstraction, simple\n   211\tdata over rich types.\n   212\t\n   213\tWhat was missing is not a mechanism but a statement: which keys the engine\n   214\towns, what the restore signature is, and the two composed forms every VM\n   215\twas writing by hand. This document is the statement; section 7 is the two\n   216\tforms.\n   217\t\n   218\t## 7. Proposed engine edits (B4's file box)\n   219\t\n   220\tAll additive, all inside `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc` and\n   221\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc`, none changing an effect rule or an outcome:\n   222\t\n   223\t1. `engine/scheduler-round [state restore-fn]`: `check-wait-set`, then\n   224\t   `resume-from-run-queue` with `restore-fn`, returning the polled state when\n   225\t   nothing woke. This is `yin.vm.semantic/scheduler-round` moved verbatim\n   226\t   with its restore made a parameter.\n   227\t\n   228\t2. One restore arity. `resume-from-run-queue` calls\n   229\t   `(restore-fn base entry (:value entry))` so that both engine call sites\n   230\t   pass three arguments. The existing two-arity shims in the semantic VM and\n   231\t   the walker keep working and may be deleted later; the fake restore in\n   232\t   `test/yin/vm/engine_test.cljc` gains a third parameter.\n   233\t\n   234\t3. `ffi/response-wait-entry [entry call-id]`: the call-out reader entry for\n   235\t   an arbitrary register payload, built by removing `:request-sent`, `:op`,\n   236\t   and `:datom` and adding `:call-id`, `:reason :next`, the call-out\n   237\t   `:cursor-ref`, and `:stream-id`. `ffi/call-response-wait-entry` (the\n   238\t   walker's frame-typed shape) is unchanged.\n   239\t\n   240\tTwo tests: `scheduler-round` returns the polled state when nothing wakes and\n   241\tthe restored state when something does; `response-wait-entry` preserves an\n   242\tarbitrary register payload verbatim.\n   243\t\n   244\tB4 consumes all three. The semantic VM and the walker are not migrated in\n   245\tB4: they are B4's parity oracles and must not move while B4 is measured\n   246\tagainst them. Migrating them afterwards is a separate, behavior-preserving\n   247\tcommit (delete `scheduler-round`, `resume-from-run-queue`, and\n   248\t`response-wait-entry` from `semantic.cljc`; delete `resume-from-run-queue`\n   249\tand the inline round from `ast_walker.cljc`) and is the owner's call.\n   250\t\n   251\t## 8. Findings outside B4's scope\n   252\t\n   253\t- `yin.vm.ast_walker` passes `{:restore-fn ast-walker-restore}` into\n   254\t  `engine/handle-effect` at six sites. The v2 engine destructures only\n   255\t  `:park-entry-fns` and ignores it. This is v1 plumbing that survived the\n   256\t  port; `yin.vm.semantic.md` section 3.5 states the rule the walker does not\n   257\t  yet follow. Harmless, and B4 must not copy it.\n   258\t- `ready-for-ingress?` speaks the named register vocabulary (section 1).\n   259\t- The semantic VM's private `response-wait-entry` becomes redundant with\n   260\t  edit 3 above.\n   130\t# COMPILATION AS STREAM PROCESSING\n   131\t\n   132\tCompilation is a stream processor. Each stage consumes a datom stream and emits a new one.\n   133\tThe compilation pipeline does not destroy or consume its input: all intermediate streams persist.\n   134\t\n   135\tPipeline topology:\n   136\t\n   137\t  Source text\n   138\t    -> yang/compile: text -> Universal AST (maps with :type, :operator, :operands, etc.)\n   139\t    -> vm/ast->datoms: AST -> datom stream [e a v t m] with :yin/ attributes and negative tempids\n   140\t    -> Three independent projections from the same datom stream:\n   141\t       1. asm/compile: datoms -> semantic bytecode (:op/ triples, queryable via Datalog)\n   142\t       2. stack/ast-datoms->asm: datoms -> stack assembly ([:push v] [:load x] [:call n])\n   143\t       3. register/ast-datoms->asm: datoms -> register assembly ([:loadk rd v] [:call rd rf args])\n   144\t    -> Assembly -> bytecode: numeric encoding with constant pools\n   145\t    -> VM execution: bytecode stream -> value + effect descriptors\n   146\t    -> AST execution: universal AST -> walker/run -> value + effect descriptors\n   147\t\n   148\tSame datoms, multiple interpreters.\n   149\tThe datom stream from ast->datoms is the shared substrate.\n   150\tEach backend (semantic, stack, register) projects the same facts into a different execution model.\n   151\tThis is the physics metaphor in practice: same wave function, different measurements.\n   152\t\n   153\tDatom stream at the AST boundary (concrete example, (+ 1 2)):\n   154\t  [-1 :yin/type :application 0 0]\n   155\t  [-1 :yin/operator -2 0 0]\n   156\t  [-1 :yin/operands [-3 -4] 0 0]\n   157\t  [-2 :yin/type :variable 0 0]\n   158\t  [-2 :yin/name + 0 0]\n   159\t  [-3 :yin/type :literal 0 0]\n   160\t  [-3 :yin/value 1 0 0]\n   161\t  [-4 :yin/type :literal 0 0]\n   162\t  [-4 :yin/value 2 0 0]\n   163\t\n   164\tSemantic projection of the same expression:\n   165\t  [-19 :op/type :apply]\n   166\t  [-19 :op/operator-node -16]\n   167\t  [-19 :op/operand-nodes [-17 -18]]\n   168\t  [-16 :op/type :load-var]\n   169\t  [-16 :op/var-name +]\n   170\t  [-17 :op/type :literal]\n   171\t  [-17 :op/value 1]\n   172\t  [-18 :op/type :literal]\n   173\t  [-18 :op/value 2]\n   174\t\n   175\tInvariant: every stage is a pure function from stream to stream.\n   176\tNo stage mutates its input. Intermediate representations coexist.\n   177\tThe AST persists alongside bytecode, alongside execution state.\n   178\t\n   179\tVisual reference: src/cljs/datomworld/demo.cljs renders this pipeline as draggable cards\n   180\tconnected by bezier curves. Each card is a stream stage. The connection-line buttons\n   181\t(AST ->, Asm ->, Reg ->, Stack ->) are the transducers. The UI makes the stream\n   182\ttopology explicit and observable.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md | sed -n '220,340p;490,580p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '360,455p'\nrg -n \\\"shared|eid|entity|occurr|memo|side table|side-table|lift|validate|validator|resolved\\\" docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md | sed -n '1,240p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md | sed -n '220,340p;490,580p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '360,455p'\nrg -n \\\"shared|eid|entity|occurr|memo|side table|side-table|lift|validate|validator|resolved\\\" docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md | sed -n '1,240p'\"","aggregated_output":"   220\tLowering is two stages, both pure stream-to-stream functions:\n   221\t\n   222\t    (resolve named-datoms)          -> resolved tuples + binder side table\n   223\t    (lower-stack resolved-tuples)   -> canonical stack image + pc side table\n   224\t\n   225\t`resolve` is the de Bruijn encoding of the named semantic tuples. It is the\n   226\tshared upstream of the stack projection specified here and the register\n   227\tprojection specified in `yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`; neither projection\n   228\tis derived from the other, matching the parallel-projection topology in\n   229\t`docs/agents/architecture.md`. `resolve` receives complete `:yin/*` named\n   230\tdatoms and reuses only the public `yin.vm.debruijn/resolve-name` helper.\n   231\tThat helper compares names exactly, including rightmost-wins duplicate\n   232\tparameters, and does not canonicalize values. Projection-only helpers such\n   233\tas `index-frame`, `build-node`, and `project-node` are not reused.\n   234\tUnexpanded-macro validation remains the named front end's responsibility.\n   235\tThe projection is therefore kept, not removed: if it is ever retired, this\n   236\tpublic helper must first move into the resolver's namespace.\n   237\t\n   238\t### 3.1 Resolved tuples\n   239\t\n   240\tThe resolved tuples are the named datoms with exactly two changes, the same\n   241\ttwo changes the B1 opcode table makes to the named instruction table. Every\n   242\tother datom is carried unchanged: entity ids, `:yin/type`, operands, exact\n   243\tscalar values, exact free names, `:yin/tail?`, and node structure. Entity\n   244\tids are preserved, so a resolved node's provenance is its own id and no\n   245\tseparate provenance table exists at this level.\n   246\t\n   247\t1. A `:variable` node loses its `:yin/name` datom and gains either\n   248\t   `:yin.resolved/depth` and `:yin.resolved/position` (a bound reference,\n   249\t   from `resolve-name`'s `{:bound [depth position]}`) or `:yin.resolved/free`\n   250\t   carrying the exact symbol (from `{:free name}`).\n   251\t2. A `:lambda` node loses its `:yin/params` datom and gains\n   252\t   `:yin.resolved/arity`, the parameter count.\n   253\t\n   254\tThe attribute namespace is `:yin.resolved/*`, not `:yin.debruijn/*`, which\n   255\tthe dormant projection owns. The binder side table is `{lambda-entity\n   256\t[param-symbol ...]}`: the exact parameter vector of every lambda, keyed by\n   257\tits entity. No name is recorded per variable occurrence; a bound occurrence's\n   258\toriginal spelling is the owning lambda's parameter at the resolved position,\n   259\twhich `resolve-name`'s contract guarantees is exact.\n   260\t\n   261\tThe walk is a tree walk in the named linearizer's order: operator then\n   262\toperands left to right, `if` test then arms, lambda bodies with the lambda's\n   263\tparameter vector pushed innermost-first on the resolution stack for the\n   264\tbody's extent. Depth 0 is the innermost enclosing lambda. This stack is\n   265\tthe resolver's only state; the runtime frame vector's outermost-first order\n   266\tis B3's, and the conversion between them is explicit there.\n   267\t\n   268\t`resolve` refuses with a qualified diagnostic, before any lowerer runs, a\n   269\tprogram whose bound reference falls outside its enclosing arity chain, an\n   270\tunsupported node type, or a host value in a data operand. A hand-built\n   271\tresolved tuple set with `:yin.resolved/depth 5` and no enclosing lambda is\n   272\trejected by `resolve`'s own validator, which walks the tree once more\n   273\tagainst the arity chain.\n   274\t\n   275\tThe resolved tuples are a derived stage value. They have this shape\n   276\tcontract and namespace so that two lowerers and their tests agree on what\n   277\tthey consume, but no hash, no identity, and no sharing role: H and R are\n   278\tthe only executable identities (D9, D12), the tuples are not executable,\n   279\tand \"derive, do not persist\" forbids a persisted copy of facts the named\n   280\tdatoms already hold. They are not the second lossless view D2 rejects:\n   281\tnever stored as a source view, never served, never a fetch key. With the\n   282\tbinder side table they invert to the named datoms; that inverse is the\n   283\tresolver's test oracle only.\n   284\t\n   285\t### 3.2 Stack lowering\n   286\t\n   287\t`lower-stack` consumes resolved tuples and emits the canonical positional\n   288\t`:yin.debruijn.code/*` image (section 2) plus the pc-keyed diagnostic side\n   289\ttable the lift needs. It cannot call `yin.vm.linearize/lower`, which reads\n   290\t`:yin/name` and `:yin/params`. It therefore reproduces the named\n   291\tlinearizer's flattening itself: the same recursion in evaluation order, the\n   292\tsame `:push` after operator and after each operand, `:call` with argc and\n   293\tthe copied `:yin/tail?`, `if` as `branch-false`, `jump`, and two labels,\n   294\t`:halt` after the root, lambda bodies out of line in discovery order each\n   295\tending in `:return`, and labels resolved to pcs in a second pass. A\n   296\t`:variable` emits `[:load-bound depth position]` or `[:load-free name]`\n   297\tstraight from its resolved attributes; a `:lambda` emits `[:closure arity\n   298\tbody-pc]`. Every other node emits the carried mnemonic unchanged.\n   299\t\n   300\tLayout equality with the named linearizer no longer holds by construction.\n   301\tIt is a required structural-comparison test: for every corpus program the\n   302\tcanonical vector of `(lower x)` and `(lower-stack (resolve x))` have the\n   303\tsame length and the same mnemonic at every pc, and differ only at `:var`\n   304\tversus `:load-bound`/`:load-free` operands and at `:closure`'s parameter\n   305\tversus arity operand. A layout change in `yin.vm.linearize` must fail this\n   306\ttest rather than silently fork H.\n   307\t\n   308\t`lower-stack` copies `:yin/tail?` exactly as it was resolved. It does not\n   309\tinfer tail position from syntax. Only `:application` has a tail operand in\n   310\tthe existing instruction table; `:dao.stream.apply/call` lowers to\n   311\t`:ffi-call` and has no tail operand.\n   312\t\n   313\tThe pc side table is `{pc {:kind :closure, :params [sym ...], :source e}}`\n   314\tfor every `:closure` pc, read from the binder side table by lambda entity,\n   315\tand `{pc {:kind :var, :source e}}` for every load pc. `adapt` is the\n   316\tcomposition `lower-stack` after `resolve` and is the one public entry that\n   317\ttakes named datoms to an image.\n   318\t\n   319\tThis removes the fused design's vector-level scope reconstruction:\n   320\t`closure-body-ranges`, `layout-conforms?`, `body-owner`, `chain-of`, and\n   321\tthe vector-level `resolve-var` and `rewrite`. That machinery existed only\n   322\tbecause resolution was placed after linearization and had to recover body\n   323\tscopes from an already flattened vector. Scope is now known during the tree\n   324\twalk, and B1's validator remains the independent vector-level check.\n   325\t\n   326\tThe executable image may carry a debug node hash and named source reference.\n   327\tThose fields are always diagnostic metadata outside code identity. They replace\n   328\tno named source map.\n   329\t\n   330\tScope validation is mandatory in both places where an image can enter:\n   331\t\n   332\t1. `resolve` validates nonnegative depth and position against the enclosing\n   333\t   arity chain at resolution time, on the tree.\n   334\t2. B1's image validator repeats the check by walking each body's declared\n   335\t   arity and enclosing-body chain. A shape-valid hand-built image with\n   336\t   `[:load-bound [5 0]]` is rejected before execution.\n   337\t\n   338\tNo projected reader participates in this path; only the B1 image validator\n   339\tadmits executable images.\n   340\t\n   490\trows,\n   491\tout-of-range bound operands, exact spelling preservation, distinct hashes\n   492\tfor composed and decomposed e-acute, a ratio fixture that is either\n   493\tencoded or refused with `:unsupported-value` (JVM encodes, CLJS and\n   494\tClojureDart both refuse), distinct hashes for `1`/`1.0` on JVM and Dart,\n   495\tdistinct hashes for ratios and chars on the JVM, and identical bytes\n   496\tacross hosts for the common scalar domain. Golden image bytes and H\n   497\tvalues for a frozen corpus are checked on all three host lanes. A\n   498\tlowering layout change must fail those fixtures rather than silently\n   499\tforking identity.\n   500\t\n   501\t### B2: resolver and stack lowerer\n   502\t\n   503\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_resolve.cljc\n   504\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_resolve_test.cljc\n   505\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc\n   506\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize_test.cljc\n   507\t    Existing edits: none\n   508\t    Must not change: yin.vm.linearize and named lowering semantics\n   509\t\n   510\tB2 is two namespaces (section 3). `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve` owns `resolve`:\n   511\tnamed datoms to resolved tuples plus binder side table, its scope validator,\n   512\tand the inverse `unresolve` used as a test oracle. It is shared with the\n   513\tregister design and depends on nothing in this design below section 3.1.\n   514\t`yin.vm.debruijn-linearize` owns `lower-stack`, `adapt`, `lift`, and the\n   515\tpublic `named-canonical-vector` helper the structural comparison uses. The\n   516\tfused implementation's `closure-body-ranges`, `layout-conforms?`,\n   517\t`body-owner`, `chain-of`, vector-level `resolve-var`, `rewrite`, and the\n   518\tduplicate vector-level `image-scope-defect` are removed, not moved: scope\n   519\tis known on the tree, and B1's validator is the vector-level check.\n   520\t\n   521\tCompletion requires, for the resolver: deterministic output on every host,\n   522\tevery node type resolved or refused with a named diagnostic, exact free\n   523\tnames and scalars, the duplicate-parameter fixture `(fn [x x] x)` resolving\n   524\tto `[0 1]`, out-of-range hand-built resolved tuples refused, and\n   525\t`unresolve(resolve x, side-table) = x` over the corpus.\n   526\t\n   527\tFor the stack lowerer: deterministic output, every node and opcode, the\n   528\tsection 3.2 structural opcode-by-opcode comparison with `lower` differing\n   529\tonly at variable and closure operands, image encode/validate/load round\n   530\ttrips, and `lift(adapt x, side-table) = canonical-vector(lower x)`. The lift\n   531\tis a function of the image plus its pc side table. With the original binder\n   532\tnames in the side table, equality holds; with synthesized names, the lifted\n   533\tresult is alpha-equivalent to `lower x`, including `(fn [x x] x)`.\n   534\tSynthesized names must be fresh against the image's free-name set. A\n   535\tsupplied side table is accepted only when `adapt` of its lift returns the\n   536\toriginal image. Every image B2 emits must be accepted by B1's validator, and\n   537\tevery hand-built out-of-range image must be rejected by B1's validator. The\n   538\tgolden image bytes and H values frozen in B1 must be reproduced unchanged\n   539\tby `adapt`; the refactor changes derivation, not bytes.\n   540\t\n   541\t### B3: de Bruijn VM kernel\n   542\t\n   543\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc\n   544\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_test.cljc\n   545\t    Existing edits: none\n   546\t    Must not change: semantic VM, engine, IVM protocols, named environment,\n   547\t    merged projection namespace\n   548\t\n   549\tImplement frames, closures, loads, calls, returns, branches, literals, and\n   550\tstore operations. Completion requires pure-program parity using B0's\n   551\tnormalizer and fresh initial environments, plus the informational section 1\n   552\tbenchmark report, not an acceptance condition.\n   553\t\n   554\t### B4: effects and continuations\n   555\t\n   556\t    Existing source: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc\n   557\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_effects_test.cljc\n   558\t    Existing edits: src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc (additive only:\n   559\t      scheduler-round; resume-from-run-queue calls restore-fn with three\n   560\t      arguments), src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc (additive only:\n   561\t      response-wait-entry), test/yin/vm/engine_test.cljc (fake restore\n   562\t      arity; tests for the two additions)\n   563\t    Must not change: dao.stream protocols, lease, waitset, named effect rules,\n   564\t    merged projection namespace, yin.vm.semantic, yin.vm.ast_walker (B4's\n   565\t    parity oracles; their migration to the engine additions is a separate\n   566\t    commit), any existing engine outcome or entry key\n   567\t\n   568\tImplement stream operations, primitives, FFI, gensym, current-continuation,\n   569\tpark, and resume against the engine seam in section 4.1 and\n   570\t`yin.vm.engine.md`. The engine edits are exactly the three listed in that\n   571\tdocument's section 7; B4 adds no other engine function and hand-writes no\n   572\tscheduler round, run-queue wrapper, or two-arity restore shim. Completion\n   573\trequires parity for values, errors, effects, stores, stream outcomes, and\n   574\tblocked states. Cross-program park/resume tests must use only\n   575\tinitial-environment or store-resolved free names until the named-VM\n   576\tenvironment leak is fixed. Before a continuation is exported, B4 must either\n   577\tlift frames through the descriptor morphism or run the frame-aware\n   578\tcompletion adapter so `:yin.k/requires` is not under-approximated.\n   579\t\n   580\t### B5: differential integration\n   360\t## 6. Implementation phases\n   361\t\n   362\tEach phase has a bounded file box, must-not-change list, completion criteria,\n   363\tand JVM, CLJS, CLJD, kondo, and cljstyle verification.\n   364\t\n   365\t### R0: contract and corpus\n   366\t\n   367\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_contract_test.cljc\n   368\t    Existing edits: none\n   369\t    Must not change: B0-B3, named datoms, stack image, resolver contract,\n   370\t    projection namespace\n   371\t\n   372\tThe resolver (`yin.vm.debruijn-resolve`: resolved tuples, side table,\n   373\tscope validation, the inverse used by tests) is now B2's own file box, per\n   374\tthe stack design's B2 phase box (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` section 3.1) --\n   375\tnot built here. R0 depends on it and freezes the register descriptor\n   376\tversion, operand mapping, normalizer fixtures, and the named-root corpus\n   377\tagainst it. Completion requires every node type from the resolver either\n   378\tmapped or refused with a named diagnostic, the duplicate-parameter and\n   379\tfree-name fixtures from B2, the section 2.2 address law between B2's stack\n   380\timages and the resolved tuples over the B0 parity corpus, pure data\n   381\tchecks, and a decision that no register kernel is assumed yet.\n   382\t\n   383\t### R1: register dimension and lowerer\n   384\t\n   385\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc\n   386\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile.cljc\n   387\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile_test.cljc\n   388\t    Existing edits: none\n   389\t    Must not change: B1 code, B2 lowerer, B3 VM, resolver contract,\n   390\t    dao.stream protocols\n   391\t\n   392\tImplement the descriptor, validation, deterministic tree lowering,\n   393\tallocation, encoding, R, and the lift to `:yin.code/*`. Completion requires\n   394\tevery resolved node type either mapped or refused with a named diagnostic,\n   395\tbyte-identical repeated lowerings, golden register vectors, cross-host R\n   396\tagreement, the section 2.2 address law extended to the register image, and\n   397\tthe lift law `lift(lower-register (resolve x), side-table)` alpha-equivalent\n   398\tto `canonical-vector(lower x)` under the arity and layout caveats of the\n   399\tstack design's B2 box.\n   400\t\n   401\t### R2: effects and stream forms\n   402\t\n   403\t    Existing source: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile.cljc\n   404\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects_test.cljc\n   405\t    Existing edits: none\n   406\t    Must not change: dao.stream, lease, waitset, B0-B3 semantics\n   407\t\n   408\tLower stream, gensym, FFI, park, and resume shapes with explicit registers.\n   409\tCompletion compares effect descriptors and blocked outcomes with the stack\n   410\tpath without executing a register VM.\n   411\t\n   412\t### R3: benchmark gate\n   413\t\n   414\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_benchmark_test.cljc\n   415\t    Existing edits: none\n   416\t    Must not change: stack VM and its H\n   417\t\n   418\tRun identical pure-program corpora through the stack VM and a reference\n   419\tregister interpreter or instrumentation harness. Report throughput,\n   420\tallocation, image size, load time, and lowering cost. A kernel is not\n   421\tauthorized merely because R1 passes; the report must show a material benefit\n   422\tor the register path remains a lowerer-only artifact.\n   423\t\n   424\t### R4: optional register kernel\n   425\t\n   426\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc\n   427\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn/register_test.cljc\n   428\t    Existing edits: none\n   429\t    Must not change: B0-B3, named VM, existing IVM methods\n   430\t\n   431\tImplement only if R3 justifies it. Completion requires B0-normalized parity\n   432\tagainst the named VM and the stack VM, all register validator fixtures,\n   433\tlexical and closure tests, store and control flow tests, and deterministic\n   434\tstream/effect behavior.\n   435\t\n   436\t### R5: linker integration\n   437\t\n   438\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_linker.cljc\n   439\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_linker_test.cljc\n   440\t    Existing edits: none\n   441\t    Must not change: stack linker semantics or stack H\n   442\t\n   443\tFetch and verify R over `dao.stream` with the same request, wire-hash,\n   444\tclosure-check, and refusal shape as B6, keyed by R. A host may refuse R and\n   445\tinstead request, by H, a stack image the composition has published for the\n   446\tsame named root; that pairing is composition data, not linker machinery. No\n   447\tglobal loader or callback is introduced.\n   448\t\n   449\t## 7. Non-goals and protected surfaces\n   450\t\n   451\tThis design does not alter any B0-B7 phase's semantics, the stack VM, named\n   452\tdatoms, the dormant projection, or the AST-walker. It does not change B1's\n   453\tH, its scalar bytes, its validator, or its wire protocol. It does not add\n   454\tequality saturation, a JIT, lambda lifting, ANF, global distribution, or a\n   455\tnew continuation interchange format. Its one proposed edit to the stack\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md:19:execution artifact and the sole sharing identity, H. The merged projection is\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md:34:in the diagnostic side table used by the executable image, while lexical\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md:44:references compare by identity; host handles and telemetry are excluded.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md:63:passes trivially. Otherwise B6 refuses it with `:unresolved-free` or\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md:74:identity, stack-trace text, source-map formatting, or interchangeability of\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md:90:    | I: share executabl