{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-22 09:47:38 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-22 16:47:38 +07 (Indochina Time)\n\n## Findings\n\nblocking | docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md:240 | Invariant 6, no assumed graphs: resolved tuples preserve source entity IDs while replacing each variable’s name with a context-dependent lexical address. The document nevertheless assumes a tree walk at lines 261-266. If one variable entity is referenced under two lexical contexts, that entity may be bound at different depths or be bound in one occurrence and free in another. A single preserved entity cannot contain both resolutions without ambiguous repeated attributes. The register design repeats the assumption at `yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:127-149,264-270`. | Give resolved occurrences deterministic identities keyed at least by source entity plus lexical context and occurrence path, while retaining the source entity as provenance. Alternatively, make context part of every resolved reference. Both lowerers and `unresolve` must consume that occurrence-aware form. Add a shared-node fixture used beneath different binders, including bound-versus-free and different-depth cases. This must be corrected before either resolved-tuples contract is signed off.\n\nblocking | docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:92-111,234-242 | The entry contract distinguishes engine-owned wake disposition from VM payload, but `response-wait-entry` removes only `:request-sent`, `:op`, and `:datom`. A woken writer may also carry stale `:value`, `:status`, `:store-updates`, and `:cursor`. Re-parking that entry can preserve or later reapply a completed wake’s disposition and store transition. This contradicts the document’s own disjoint-key contract and explicit-transition requirement. | Define the helper as a projection from the old VM payload, not a partial mutation of the ready entry. Remove every engine-owned wake/result key before adding the new reader keys, while preserving only the VM register payload and necessary FFI correlation data. Add a test starting from a deliberately contaminated ready entry and prove that arbitrary VM payload survives while all prior wake disposition is absent. Update the B4 wording accordingly.\n\nshould-fix | docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md:268-273,287-324 | Splitting validation from lowering leaves a public seam: `resolve` validates, but `lower-stack` merely “consumes resolved tuples.” A caller can bypass `resolve` with hand-built data. B1 will reject a malformed final image, but the stage contract itself does not guarantee that every lowerer invokes the resolved-form validator. The register design is internally inconsistent here: it promises refusal at lines 120-123 but limits its own checks to register/body/target bounds at lines 258-271. | Export one pure `validate-resolved` function from the resolver and require both `lower-stack` and `lower-register` to call it unconditionally. Direct-lowerer tests must show identical qualified refusal for malformed context, conflicting facts, and out-of-range lexical addresses. B1 and the register-image validator remain independent admission checks.\n\nshould-fix | docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:70-77 | H and R are correctly independent, but the claimed biconditional, “share H iff share R,” does not follow merely because both are pure functions of the same input. Different deterministic projections may preserve different distinctions. A corpus test supplies evidence, not a general identity proof. | Remove the biconditional and state only that each format is deterministically derived and independently addressed. If exact equivalence is required, define a canonical stack/register reconstruction law strong enough to prove it and make that law part of the descriptors’ contracts. The H/R pairing may remain composition data.\n\nshould-fix | docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:115-118,249-252,383-399 | Binder names live in a side table outside R, yet the lift consumes that unverified table. A supplied table can capture a free name or otherwise change the named image executed through the semantic VM. The stack design already specifies the missing safeguard at `yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md:530-536`. | Make synthesized names, fresh against all free names, the default execution lift. Accept a supplied diagnostic table only when its arities are valid, its names are capture-free, and lowering the lifted result reproduces the original register image. Add original-name equality and synthesized-name alpha-equivalence tests, including duplicate parameters.\n\n## Properties that passed\n\n- The peer-projection topology is sound. Stack H and register R have independent preimages, and neither executable format depends on the other.\n- Giving resolved tuples no hash or sharing identity is correct. They are derived, non-executable stage data. A receiver holding R verifies, validates, and executes or lifts the register image without fetching resolved tuples.\n- The stream-processing boundary remains pure and non-destructive. Named datoms and executable images coexist without hidden global state or mutation.\n- The B2 split retains the necessary intended end conditions: structural comparison with named lowering, the lift law, B1 validation, and reproduction of the frozen H fixtures.\n- Moving `scheduler-round` into the engine and standardizing the three-argument restore call are sound, additive changes.\n- A formal VM protocol is not warranted. The stable bare-function restore convention has actual three-VM precedent, while park-entry builders necessarily close over instruction-site data. The documented key contract is the appropriate abstraction boundary.\n- Leaving park-entry construction at VM instruction sites does not create three copies of scheduler glue. After extracting `scheduler-round`, the shared responsibility ends at scheduling and entry conventions; the remaining builders express genuinely VM-specific register capture.\n- The explicit state maps, host-neutral data contracts, CLJ/CLJS/CLJD verification requirements, and protected-surface lists conform to the foundational invariants.\n- Deferring the register kernel, B4 implementation, and migration of existing VMs to `engine/scheduler-round` is architecturally acceptable.\n\n## Verdicts\n\n- `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`: **not sound** as written. The peer topology is sound, but its shared resolved artifact cannot represent context-dependent occurrences safely.\n- `docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`: **not sound** as written. The occurrence-identity defect is blocking; the direct-lowerer validation seam also needs closure.\n- `docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md`: **sound with changes**. The scheduler boundary and no-protocol decision are sound, but `response-wait-entry` must remove stale engine-owned disposition data.\n\nOverall sign-off: **WITHHELD** pending the occurrence-aware resolved-tuple correction and safe FFI re-park construction."}}
